/* Black history figures: 5 daily lessons each (WHO/WHAT/WHEN/WHY/HOW) with 10-question quizzes. Review for accuracy before use. */
window.BLACK_HISTORY=[{"id":"douglass","name":"Frederick Douglass","years":"1818-1895","era":"past","tagline":"Escaped slavery and became the most powerful voice against it","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"Born Into Slavery, Hungry to Learn","paras":["Frederick Douglass was born into slavery in Talbot County, Maryland, around 1818. His birth name was Frederick Bailey, and the exact date of his birth is unknown. As a baby, he was separated from his mother, Harriet Bailey. His grandmother, Betty Bailey, raised him until he was about seven.","When Frederick was about twelve, he was sent to Baltimore to work for the Auld family. Sophia Auld began teaching him the alphabet. Her husband, Hugh Auld, did not approve, so the lessons stopped. Frederick did not give up and kept learning to read on his own.","Frederick found a book called The Columbian Orator. It was full of speeches and essays about freedom. He studied it closely, again and again, and learned how powerful words can be. The book helped him shape his own ideas about freedom, and reading became his secret weapon.","As a teenager, Frederick worked in a Baltimore shipyard as a caulker. A caulker seals the seams of wooden ships so water cannot get in. It was hard, skilled work, and like other enslaved workers he had to hand over his pay. That was deeply unfair.","After he escaped slavery in 1838, Frederick needed a new name so he would not be caught. A friend named Nathan Johnson suggested the name Douglass. It came from a poem called The Lady of the Lake. Frederick kept his first name and became Frederick Douglass."],"remember":"Frederick Douglass was born enslaved, taught himself to read, and chose his own name as a free man.","quiz":[["Where was Frederick Douglass born?","Talbot County, Maryland","Richmond, Virginia","Boston, Massachusetts","Savannah, Georgia",0],["Who raised him until he was about seven?","His grandmother, Betty Bailey","His teacher, Sophia Auld","His friend, Nathan Johnson","His boss, Hugh Auld",0],["Who began teaching Frederick the alphabet?","Sophia Auld","Betty Bailey","Nathan Johnson","Harriet Bailey",1],["To which city was he sent at about age twelve?","Baltimore","Boston","Richmond","Rochester",1],["What was the name of the book he found?","The Columbian Orator","The Liberator","The North Star","The Auld Reader",2],["What did the book contain?","Speeches and essays about freedom","Maps of the Northern states","Stories about famous sailors","Rules for playing baseball",2],["What was his job in a Baltimore shipyard?","A caulker","A carpenter","A cook","A ship captain",3],["What does a caulker seal?","The seams of wooden ships","The roofs of wooden houses","The pages of old books","The wheels of train cars",3],["Who suggested the name Douglass?","Nathan Johnson","Hugh Auld","Betty Bailey","Sophia Auld",4],["What poem did the name Douglass come from?","The Lady of the Lake","The Columbian Orator","The Auld Family Story","The Shipyard Song",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"Escape, Speeches, and a Famous Book","paras":["On September 3, 1838, Frederick Douglass escaped slavery. He wore a sailor's uniform and carried papers from a free Black sailor. He rode a train toward Havre de Grace, Maryland, and then went on to New York. Soon he settled in New Bedford, Massachusetts, as a free man.","In 1841, Douglass met William Lloyd Garrison, who ran an anti-slavery newspaper called The Liberator. Garrison encouraged Douglass to tell his story in public. Douglass became a traveling speaker for anti-slavery groups across the North, and word of him spread quickly. Crowds were amazed by his clear and powerful speeches.","In 1845, Douglass published a book about his life called Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave. It became an instant success and made Douglass famous. Readers learned what slavery was really like from someone who had lived it. Many were moved to join the fight against it.","Douglass then spent two years in Britain. English admirers arranged to buy his freedom while he was there. Back home, he started his own newspaper, the North Star, in Rochester, New York. Now he could share his ideas in his own voice, on his own terms.","During the Civil War, Douglass pushed President Abraham Lincoln to make ending slavery a goal of the war. He also urged the government to let Black men fight as soldiers. His sons Charles and Lewis joined the 54th Massachusetts. It was one of the first Black regiments."],"remember":"Douglass escaped slavery, then used speeches, a bestselling book, and a newspaper to fight it.","quiz":[["What did Douglass wear during his escape in 1838?","A sailor's uniform","A soldier's coat","A farmer's overalls","A preacher's robe",0],["Where did he settle as a free man?","New Bedford, Massachusetts","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Rochester, New York","Baltimore, Maryland",0],["What was the name of Garrison's newspaper?","The Liberator","The North Star","The Narrative","The Orator",1],["What did Garrison encourage Douglass to do?","Tell his story in public","Open a shipyard","Move to Britain","Join the army",1],["In what year did Douglass publish his famous book?","1845","1838","1852","1863",2],["How did readers react to the book?","It became an instant success","Few people read it","It was banned at once","It was only a pamphlet",2],["How long did Douglass stay in Britain?","Two years","Two weeks","Ten years","Six months",3],["In which city did he start the North Star?","Rochester, New York","New Bedford, Massachusetts","Washington, D.C.","Baltimore, Maryland",3],["Which President did Douglass urge to make ending slavery a war goal?","Abraham Lincoln","George Washington","Andrew Johnson","Benjamin Harrison",4],["Which regiment did his sons Charles and Lewis join?","The 54th Massachusetts","The 1st Maryland","The 7th New York","The 20th Ohio",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"A Life in Order: 1818 to 1895","paras":["Frederick Douglass was born around 1818 in Talbot County, Maryland. At about twelve years old, he was sent to Baltimore, where he began learning to read, which changed his life. As a teenager, he worked in a Baltimore shipyard. He was still enslaved all this time.","On September 3, 1838, Douglass escaped slavery at about twenty years old. He soon settled in New Bedford, Massachusetts. In 1841, he began speaking at anti-slavery meetings and met William Lloyd Garrison. That was the start of his career as one of America's best-known public speakers.","His book, Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, came out in 1845. Soon after, he traveled to Britain and stayed about two years. English admirers arranged to buy his freedom there. In 1847, he returned to the United States and started his own newspaper, the North Star.","In 1848, Douglass attended the first women's rights convention in Seneca Falls, New York. In 1852, he gave a famous speech in Rochester about the Fourth of July. The Civil War began in 1861. In 1863, he wrote a call titled Men of Color, to Arms!","In 1877, Douglass became the United States Marshal for Washington, D.C. In 1889, President Benjamin Harrison sent him to Haiti as a diplomat. Douglass died in Washington, D.C., on February 20, 1895. He is buried in Mount Hope Cemetery in Rochester, New York, a city where he lived for many years."],"remember":"Born around 1818, free in 1838, famous author by 1845, and a leader until he died in 1895.","quiz":[["About how old was Douglass when he was sent to Baltimore?","About twelve","About five","About sixteen","About twenty",0],["Around what year was Douglass born?","1818","1798","1828","1838",0],["In what year did he begin speaking at anti-slavery meetings?","1841","1835","1845","1852",1],["On what date did Douglass escape slavery?","September 3, 1838","July 5, 1852","February 20, 1895","April 15, 1861",1],["How long did Douglass stay in Britain?","About two years","About two months","About ten years","About one week",2],["In which year did he start the North Star?","1847","1838","1852","1863",2],["Where was the women's rights convention held in 1848?","Seneca Falls, New York","Boston, Massachusetts","Washington, D.C.","New Bedford, Massachusetts",3],["In what year did the Civil War begin?","1861","1838","1845","1877",3],["In which year did Douglass become the U.S. Marshal for Washington, D.C.?","1877","1838","1865","1881",4],["Where did Douglass die?","Washington, D.C.","Baltimore, Maryland","New Bedford, Massachusetts","Talbot County, Maryland",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Douglass Never Stopped Fighting","paras":["Douglass lived inside slavery, so he knew how cruel it was. As a baby, he was separated from his mother. Enslaved people were forced to work without pay, and in many places in the South it was against the law to teach enslaved people to read. These unfair rules fueled his anger.","Hugh Auld once said that learning to read would make an enslaved person hard to control. Douglass heard this and made a choice. He decided that reading was the way out of slavery. The people who wanted to keep him down had shown him what mattered most.","Douglass was not satisfied with just his own freedom. He wanted others to be free too, so after escaping in 1838 he spoke at anti-slavery meetings. He shared his own story because many people did not know what slavery was truly like. Hearing it from someone who lived it was powerful.","During the Civil War, Douglass wanted the war to be about ending slavery. He believed the nation could not be truly free while people were still enslaved. He argued that Black soldiers could help the Union win the war. He also believed they would prove they deserved equal rights.","Slavery ended in 1865, but Douglass kept working. Black Americans still faced unfair treatment and often could not vote. He fought for their right to vote and supported the right of women to vote too. Douglass believed that equal rights should belong to everyone, not just a few people."],"remember":"Douglass acted because he knew slavery's pain and believed freedom and equal rights belong to everyone.","quiz":[["What was against the law in many Southern places?","To teach enslaved people to read","To sail a ship at night","To print a newspaper","To sell books",0],["As a baby, whom was Douglass separated from?","His mother","His grandmother","His sister","His teacher",0],["Who said that reading would make an enslaved person hard to control?","Hugh Auld","William Lloyd Garrison","Abraham Lincoln","Nathan Johnson",1],["What did Douglass decide reading was?","The way out of slavery","A waste of time","A skill only for sailors","Something to avoid",1],["Why did Douglass share his own story?","Many people did not know what slavery was truly like","He wanted to earn a lot of money","He was asked to by a President","He wanted to leave America",2],["What did Douglass do after escaping in 1838?","He spoke at anti-slavery meetings","He went back to the shipyard","He joined the Navy","He opened a school",2],["What did Douglass want the Civil War to be about?","Ending slavery","Building railroads","Trading goods","Winning new land",3],["What could Black soldiers help the Union do?","Win the war","Build new ships","Elect a President","Print newspapers",3],["In what year did slavery end, according to the lesson?","1865","1838","1845","1895",4],["Besides Black Americans, whose right to vote did Douglass support?","Women","Sailors","Foreign visitors","Children",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Douglass Turned Words Into Power","paras":["Douglass never waited for someone to hand him a chance. After the lessons stopped, he kept learning to read on his own. He studied books like The Columbian Orator. Over time, reading gave him a huge vocabulary, and soon he could argue about the biggest ideas of his day.","Douglass became one of the best speakers of his time. Crowds listened because he told true stories from his own life. Nobody could easily brush off a person who had lived through slavery. People came to hear him in the United States and Britain.","Douglass also used the printed word, and his Narrative was read by many people in several countries. His newspaper, the North Star, let him speak in his own voice. Later, he wrote more books about his life, and every page spread his message far beyond any room where he spoke.","Douglass talked with powerful people face to face. He met Lincoln at the White House in 1863 and 1864. He pushed Lincoln to let Black men fight for the Union. He did not wait for leaders to act, and he asked for change directly.","Douglass's home in Washington, D.C., called Cedar Hill, is now a national historic site. For an athlete, his story has a clear lesson. Douglass kept going when no one helped him. Practice even when no one is helping you; extra reps, film study, and daily effort work the same way."],"remember":"Douglass built his power with daily practice, honest stories, and the courage to ask for change.","quiz":[["What did Douglass do after the reading lessons stopped?","He kept learning to read on his own","He gave up reading","He joined a school","He asked Hugh Auld to teach him",0],["Which book did he study?","The Columbian Orator","The Lady of the Lake","The North Star","The Liberator",0],["Why did crowds listen to Douglass?","He told true stories from his own life","He gave out free tickets","He spoke only about sports","He was the President's friend",1],["Where did people come to hear him speak?","The United States and Britain","France and Spain","Canada and Mexico","Haiti and Cuba",1],["What did his newspaper let him do?","Speak in his own voice","Hide his name","Stay out of politics","Avoid public speaking",2],["What was the name of his newspaper?","The North Star","The Liberator","The Columbian Orator","The Rochester Times",2],["In which years did Douglass meet Lincoln?","1863 and 1864","1838 and 1841","1845 and 1847","1877 and 1889",3],["Where did Douglass meet Lincoln?","At the White House","At a shipyard","At a baseball game","At Cedar Hill",3],["What is Cedar Hill today?","A national historic site","A shipyard","A newspaper office","A sports stadium",4],["What lesson does the story give athletes?","Practice even when no one is helping","Skip practice when you are tired","Wait for a coach to tell you what to do","Practice only before big games",4]]}]},{"id":"robinson","name":"Jackie Robinson","years":"1919-1972","era":"past","tagline":"Broke baseball's color line and faced hate with courage","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Kid From Pasadena Who Did It All","paras":["Jackie Robinson was born on January 31, 1919, in Cairo, Georgia. His full name was Jack Roosevelt Robinson, and he was the youngest of five children. His mother, Mallie Robinson, worked hard to take care of the family. Jackie would grow up to change American sports.","In 1920, Jackie's father left the family. His mother, Mallie, moved the family to Pasadena, California, and raised the five children. Money was tight, and life in Pasadena was not always easy for a family of six. Still, the family stayed together and Jackie grew up loving sports.","Jackie came from a family of athletes. His older brother, Matthew, was a sprinter, which means he ran short, fast races. Matthew won a silver medal in the 200 meters at the 1936 Olympic Games. Jackie grew up to be a gifted athlete too, and he would become even more famous.","Jackie went to UCLA, a university in Los Angeles, and played on many of its teams. There he became the first athlete in school history to earn varsity letters, awards for playing on a top team, in four sports. The sports were baseball, basketball, football, and track. It showed how gifted he was.","In 1942, Jackie was drafted into the Army. In January 1943, he became a second lieutenant. In 1944, he faced a court-martial, an Army trial, after a dispute on a bus. A panel of nine officers found him not guilty, and he received an honorable discharge in November 1944."],"remember":"Jackie Robinson grew up in a hard-working family, starred in four sports at UCLA, and served as an Army officer.","quiz":[["Where was Jackie Robinson born?","Cairo, Georgia","Pasadena, California","Brooklyn, New York","Kansas City, Missouri",0],["What was Jackie's place in his family?","The youngest of five children","The oldest of five children","An only child","The middle of three children",0],["In what year did Jackie's father leave the family?","1920","1925","1930","1936",1],["Where did Mallie move the family?","Pasadena, California","Atlanta, Georgia","Chicago, Illinois","Montreal, Canada",1],["What kind of athlete was Jackie's brother Matthew?","A sprinter","A pitcher","A boxer","A swimmer",2],["What did Matthew win at the 1936 Olympic Games?","A silver medal in the 200 meters","A gold medal in the long jump","A bronze medal in boxing","A gold medal in the mile",2],["In how many sports did Jackie earn varsity letters at UCLA?","Four sports","Two sports","Three sports","Five sports",3],["Which sports did Jackie letter in?","Baseball, basketball, football, and track","Baseball, soccer, tennis, and golf","Football, swimming, boxing, and track","Basketball, hockey, baseball, and golf",3],["What rank did Jackie reach in the Army?","Second lieutenant","General","Colonel","Sergeant",4],["What was the result of his court-martial?","Not guilty","Fined and sent home","Sent to prison","Moved to another base",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"Breaking Baseball's Color Line","paras":["After the Army, Robinson played baseball in 1945 for the Kansas City Monarchs. The Monarchs were a team in the Negro Leagues, which were leagues for Black players. At that time, Black players were not allowed in the major leagues. Robinson hit .387 in 47 games that season.","Branch Rickey was the general manager of the Brooklyn Dodgers. He wanted to break baseball's color line, the unwritten rule that kept Black players out, and he chose Robinson to do it. Rickey wanted a player strong enough not to fight back when people insulted him. Robinson agreed to take on that challenge.","In 1946, Robinson played for the Montreal Royals, a minor league team owned by the Dodgers. He led the International League with a .349 batting average. The league named him its Most Valuable Player. That strong season showed everyone that Robinson was ready for the big leagues.","On April 15, 1947, Robinson played first base for the Brooklyn Dodgers. He broke baseball's color line in the major leagues that day. Later that year, he won the first Rookie of the Year award. It honored the best new player in all of baseball that year.","In 1949, Robinson won the National League Most Valuable Player award. He hit .342 that year, the best average in the league. He was an All-Star six times, in each season from 1949 through 1954. In 1955, the Dodgers won the World Series, which was the biggest prize in baseball."],"remember":"Robinson went from the Negro Leagues to the Dodgers, won Rookie of the Year in 1947 and MVP in 1949.","quiz":[["Which team did Robinson play for in 1945?","The Kansas City Monarchs","The Brooklyn Dodgers","The Montreal Royals","The Pasadena Pirates",0],["What was his batting average with that team?",".387",".287",".312",".401",0],["Who chose Robinson to break the color line?","Branch Rickey","Pee Wee Reese","Matthew Robinson","Mallie Robinson",1],["What kind of player did Rickey want?","A player strong enough not to fight back","A player who could hit the most home runs","A player who had played in the Army","A player who had never lost a game",1],["Which team did Robinson play for in 1946?","The Montreal Royals","The Kansas City Monarchs","The Brooklyn Dodgers","The Pasadena Pirates",2],["What batting average did he have in 1946?",".349",".249",".387",".412",2],["On what date did Robinson play his first Dodgers game?","April 15, 1947","January 31, 1919","October 24, 1972","April 15, 1962",3],["What award did Robinson win in 1947?","Rookie of the Year","Most Valuable Player","Batting Champion","Golden Glove",3],["What was Robinson's batting average in 1949?",".342",".387",".349",".298",4],["How many times was Robinson an All-Star?","Six times","Two times","Four times","Ten times",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Robinson's Life in Order","paras":["Jackie Robinson was born on January 31, 1919, in Cairo, Georgia. In 1920, his mother moved the family to Pasadena, California, after his father left. He starred in sports at UCLA as a young man. In 1942, he was drafted into the Army, and in January 1943 he became a second lieutenant.","In August 1944, Robinson faced a court-martial and was found not guilty. He received an honorable discharge in November 1944. Then in 1945, he joined the Kansas City Monarchs of the Negro Leagues and hit .387 in 47 games. Baseball was waiting for him, but not yet in the major leagues.","In 1946, Robinson played for the Montreal Royals and was named the International League's Most Valuable Player. On April 15, 1947, he took the field for the Brooklyn Dodgers. That was the day he broke the color line in the major leagues. He won Rookie of the Year later that season.","In 1949, Robinson won the National League Most Valuable Player award. He was an All-Star every year from 1949 through 1954, six seasons in a row. In 1955, the Dodgers won the World Series, which was Robinson's only championship. He retired on January 5, 1957, after ten seasons in the major leagues.","In 1962, Robinson was inducted into the Baseball Hall of Fame on his first ballot. He died on October 24, 1972, at age 53. On April 15, 1997, Major League Baseball retired his number 42 for every team. Since 2004, players have worn 42 on April 15 each year."],"remember":"Born in 1919, in the majors in 1947, MVP in 1949, Hall of Famer in 1962.","quiz":[["In what year did Jackie's family move to Pasadena?","1920","1925","1930","1935",0],["In what year was Jackie drafted into the Army?","1942","1938","1946","1950",0],["In which month of 1944 did he receive his honorable discharge?","November","January","June","March",1],["In what year did he join the Kansas City Monarchs?","1945","1941","1946","1947",1],["In which year did Robinson play for the Montreal Royals?","1946","1944","1949","1955",2],["On what date did Robinson play his first game for the Dodgers?","April 15, 1947","January 5, 1957","October 24, 1972","April 15, 1962",2],["In what year did Robinson win the National League MVP award?","1949","1947","1951","1953",3],["What happened in 1955?","The Dodgers won the World Series","Robinson was drafted","Robinson won Rookie of the Year","Robinson entered the Hall of Fame",3],["In what year was Robinson inducted into the Hall of Fame?","1962","1949","1955","1957",4],["How old was Robinson when he died?","53","41","58","70",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Robinson Said Yes","paras":["Before 1947, Black players were kept out of the major leagues. They played in separate leagues called the Negro Leagues. Many were great players, but they were not allowed to compete in the major leagues. That was unfair to the players, and baseball needed to change.","Branch Rickey wanted a player who could handle insults without fighting back. He feared a fight would give critics an excuse to keep Black players out. Robinson was a fiery competitor, but he agreed to hold his temper. He understood that other Black players were counting on him.","Robinson faced insults from some fans and some opposing players. At first, a few of his own teammates were not welcoming either. Others supported him, including shortstop Pee Wee Reese of the Dodgers. Support like that meant a great deal to a player who was standing alone.","Robinson let his play do the talking, and it spoke loudly. In 1947, he won the Rookie of the Year award. In 1949, he was named the National League MVP. He was also a daring base runner who stole home 19 times in his career.","After he stopped playing, Robinson kept working for equal rights. He served on the board of the NAACP, a civil rights group that works for equal treatment. He used his fame to speak up about fairness in America. He wanted to open doors for others, not just for himself."],"remember":"Robinson said yes because he knew his success could open the door for every player who came after him.","quiz":[["Where did Black players play before 1947?","The Negro Leagues","The Army leagues","The minor leagues only","College teams only",0],["Before which year were Black players kept out of the major leagues?","1947","1927","1937","1957",0],["What did Robinson agree to do?","Hold his temper","Stay home","Play only at night","Switch to football",1],["Why did Rickey fear a fight?","A fight would give critics an excuse","Fans would stop buying tickets","The Dodgers would lose games","Robinson might get hurt",1],["Who supported Robinson, according to the lesson?","Pee Wee Reese","Branch Rickey","Matthew Robinson","Mallie Robinson",2],["From whom did Robinson face insults?","Some fans and some opposing players","Only his coaches","Only reporters","Only his own family",2],["How many times did Robinson steal home in his career?","19 times","5 times","50 times","100 times",3],["What award did Robinson win in 1949?","National League MVP","Batting champion","Cy Young winner","Gold Glove winner",3],["Which civil rights group did Robinson serve on the board of?","The NAACP","The Dodgers","The Army","The Negro Leagues",4],["What did Robinson use his fame to do?","Speak up about fairness","Sell sports equipment","Run for President","Avoid the news",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Robinson Stayed Strong","paras":["Robinson was fast, strong, and smart. At UCLA, he lettered in baseball, basketball, football, and track, so he had many athletic skills. On the bases, his speed made pitchers nervous, and he stole home 19 times in his career. He played with energy and took chances.","Robinson's biggest challenge was holding his temper. He agreed to stay calm and not fight back during his first years in the major leagues. That took more strength than fighting would have. Later in his career, he began to speak out more openly about unfair treatment.","Robinson did not do it alone. His mother, Mallie, had raised him to be strong, and his wife, Rachel, stood by him. Branch Rickey backed him in the front office, and teammate Pee Wee Reese supported him. Great athletes need good people around them.","Robinson proved himself with results. In 1946, he led the International League in hitting with a .349 average. In 1949, he led the National League with a .342 average. Winning games and hitting well made it hard for anyone to argue he did not belong.","Every April 15, players across Major League Baseball wear number 42 on Jackie Robinson Day. The tradition began in 2004. For an athlete, his story teaches control: you cannot control every insult or bad call, but you can control how you react. Stay calm, work hard, and let your play speak."],"remember":"Robinson won by controlling his temper, leaning on people who believed in him, and letting results speak.","quiz":[["What made pitchers nervous?","His speed","His height","His loud voice","His throwing arm",0],["How many times did he steal home in his career?","19 times","3 times","40 times","90 times",0],["What was Robinson's biggest challenge?","Holding his temper","Learning to swim","Finding a bat","Getting to practice",1],["What did Robinson begin to do later in his career?","Speak out more openly","Stop playing sports","Coach football","Move to Georgia",1],["Who was Robinson's wife?","Rachel","Harriet","Sophia","Betty",2],["Who backed Robinson in the front office?","Branch Rickey","Satchel Paige","Walter Alston","Matthew Robinson",2],["Which league did Robinson lead in hitting in 1946?","The International League","The Negro Leagues","The American League","The Pacific Coast League",3],["What was his average when he led the National League?",".342",".312",".387",".298",3],["What number do players wear on Jackie Robinson Day?","42","19","47","24",4],["What can an athlete always control, according to the lesson?","How you react","Every bad call","What fans say","The weather",4]]}]},{"id":"johnson","name":"Katherine Johnson","years":"1918-2020","era":"past","tagline":"NASA mathematician whose careful math helped send Americans into space","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Girl Who Loved Numbers","paras":["Katherine Johnson was born on August 26, 1918, in White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia. From a young age, she loved numbers and showed a strong talent for math. She liked to count and to solve problems. That love of math would take her all the way to space.","Katherine was so good in school that she skipped several grades. She was far ahead of most other kids her age. She attended high school on the campus of West Virginia State College, where she kept learning more math. Teachers noticed her special gift for math.","At age 18, Katherine enrolled at West Virginia State College. She worked hard and graduated in 1937 with the highest honors. She earned her degree in mathematics and French, two very different subjects. Now she was ready to start her career in the world beyond school.","After graduating, Katherine taught at a Black public school in Virginia. She shared her love of math with her students. She also married James Goble, and the two of them had three daughters. Katherine cared deeply about both her family and her work, and she kept both going.","In 1939, West Virginia opened its graduate schools to Black students. Katherine was one of three Black students chosen to attend West Virginia University. She left after the first session. Her biggest chance to use her math skills was still ahead of her, though she did not know it yet."],"remember":"Katherine Johnson loved numbers, skipped grades, and graduated from college with highest honors in 1937.","quiz":[["Where was Katherine Johnson born?","White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia","Hampton, Virginia","Pasadena, California","Cairo, Georgia",0],["What did she love from a young age?","Numbers","Painting","Music","Horses",0],["What did Katherine do in school because she was so good?","She skipped several grades","She started a math club","She left school early","She taught her own class",1],["Where did she attend high school?","On the campus of West Virginia State College","On the campus of UCLA","At a naval shipyard","In Pasadena, California",1],["In what year did Katherine graduate from college?","1937","1933","1941","1945",2],["Which subjects did she earn her degree in?","Mathematics and French","Science and history","Music and English","Physics and art",2],["What did Katherine do after graduating?","Taught at a Black public school in Virginia","Joined NASA right away","Became an Army officer","Worked on a farm",3],["How many daughters did Katherine and James have?","Three","Four","Five","Seven",3],["How many Black students were chosen for West Virginia University in 1939?","Three","Two","Six","Twelve",4],["Which university did she attend for graduate school?","West Virginia University","West Virginia State College","Langley University","Virginia Tech",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"The Math That Reached Space","paras":["In the summer of 1953, Katherine began working at the Langley laboratory in Virginia, which was run by NACA. She joined the West Area Computing section. It was a group of Black mathematicians who did math by hand for engineers. Dorothy Vaughan led the group.","In 1958, NACA became NASA, the space agency we know today. By then, Katherine's work had earned her a place on the Space Task Group. This team worked on America's first human spaceflights. Her skills were needed for a new goal: sending people into space.","In May 1961, Alan Shepard became the first American in space. Katherine did the trajectory analysis for his flight. A trajectory is the path a spacecraft follows through air and space. Getting the path right meant the capsule would come back down where it was supposed to.","In 1962, John Glenn was set to become the first American to orbit Earth in a spacecraft called Friendship 7. Computers had already calculated his path. Glenn asked Katherine to check the numbers by hand before the flight. She did, and the mission was a success.","Katherine's math also helped the Apollo program, which sent astronauts to the Moon. She helped line up the paths of the Lunar Module and the Command and Service Module. She also worked on the path for Apollo 11, the first Moon landing. When Apollo 13 ran into trouble in 1970, her backup charts helped find a safe path home."],"remember":"Katherine Johnson's careful math helped Shepard, Glenn, and the Apollo crews fly to space and return safely.","quiz":[["Who led the West Area Computing group?","Dorothy Vaughan","Alan Shepard","John Glenn","Margot Lee Shetterly",0],["What did the group do for engineers?","Math by hand","Test airplanes","Build rockets","Fix computers",0],["What did NACA become in 1958?","NASA","NATO","NOAA","NAACP",1],["Which team did Katherine's work earn her a place on?","The Space Task Group","The Flight Safety Board","The Rocket Design Team","The Mission Control Crew",1],["Who became the first American in space in May 1961?","Alan Shepard","John Glenn","Neil Armstrong","Dorothy Vaughan",2],["What is a trajectory?","The path a spacecraft follows","The speed of a rocket","The weight of a capsule","The color of a spacecraft",2],["What did Glenn ask Katherine to do?","Check the numbers by hand","Fly the spacecraft","Build the capsule","Write a book",3],["What was the name of Glenn's spacecraft?","Friendship 7","Apollo 11","Lunar Module","Freedom 13",3],["Which NASA program sent astronauts to the Moon?","Apollo","Skylab","Voyager","Viking",4],["What did her backup charts help find for Apollo 13?","A safe path home","A new landing spot","A faster rocket","A better radio",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Johnson's Life in Order","paras":["Katherine Johnson was born on August 26, 1918, in White Sulphur Springs, West Virginia. She enrolled at West Virginia State College at age 18. She graduated with highest honors in 1937. In 1939, she was one of three Black students chosen for West Virginia University.","In the summer of 1953, Katherine joined the Langley laboratory in Virginia, where she did math for engineers. She worked there for the next 33 years. In 1958, NACA became NASA, the new space agency, and she joined the space program. Her work soon turned toward spaceflight.","In May 1961, astronaut Alan Shepard flew into space, and Katherine did the trajectory analysis for his flight. In 1962, John Glenn orbited Earth in Friendship 7. Before that flight, Glenn asked Katherine to check the computer's numbers by hand. Both missions came early in America's space race.","In 1969, Apollo 11 landed astronauts on the Moon, and Katherine's math helped plan its path. In 1970, Apollo 13 had trouble in space, and her backup charts helped guide it home. Katherine retired in 1986, after 33 years at Langley. In all, she helped write 26 research papers.","In 2015, at age 97, Katherine received the Presidential Medal of Freedom from President Barack Obama. In 2019, she was awarded the Congressional Gold Medal. Katherine Johnson died on February 24, 2020, at age 101. Her long life stretched across more than a century."],"remember":"Born in 1918, at NASA from 1953 to 1986, honored in 2015, and gone in 2020 at age 101.","quiz":[["In what year did Katherine graduate with highest honors?","1937","1933","1941","1945",0],["In what year was she chosen for West Virginia University?","1939","1935","1943","1953",0],["In which year did Katherine join the Langley laboratory?","1953","1943","1963","1973",1],["In what year did NACA become NASA?","1958","1948","1962","1969",1],["In what month of 1961 did Alan Shepard fly into space?","May","January","September","December",2],["Who orbited Earth in Friendship 7 in 1962?","John Glenn","Neil Armstrong","Dorothy Vaughan","Barack Obama",2],["In which year did Apollo 13 have trouble in space?","1970","1961","1965","1972",3],["In which year did Katherine retire?","1986","1976","1990","1996",3],["How old was Katherine when she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom?","97","87","77","107",4],["On what date did Katherine Johnson die?","February 24, 2020","March 15, 2020","February 24, 2019","June 1, 2019",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Every Number Mattered","paras":["Early spaceflights were dangerous because nobody had done them before. Astronauts had to travel through space and then come back safely. A small math mistake could send a capsule to the wrong place. That is why astronauts' lives depended on the numbers, and why every number had to be exact.","In 1962, computers were new and not everyone trusted them yet. John Glenn trusted Katherine's careful work. Before his flight, he asked her to check the computer's numbers by hand. Her skill and accuracy had earned that trust, and she did not let him down.","Katherine worked during a time of segregation. At Langley, she first worked in West Area Computing, an all-Black section of mathematicians who did math by hand. Black Americans and women often were denied chances in those years. Katherine had to prove her skills again and again.","Katherine was fascinated by numbers from a young age, and she never lost that spark. Her knowledge of analytic geometry, a branch of math that uses graphs and equations, helped her win allies among her male coworkers. Skill earned her respect, and respect opened doors for her at work.","For many years, the public knew very little about Katherine and her coworkers. Then, in 2015, President Barack Obama gave her the Presidential Medal of Freedom. Author Margot Lee Shetterly wrote the book Hidden Figures to tell the story of these women. A movie of the same name followed."],"remember":"Katherine's numbers mattered because astronauts' lives depended on them, and her skill earned lasting trust.","quiz":[["What could a small math mistake do?","Send a capsule to the wrong place","Make a rocket too heavy","Stop a radio signal","Delay a launch by a year",0],["What depended on the numbers?","Astronauts' lives","Newspaper stories","Stadium crowds","Movie ticket sales",0],["Who trusted Katherine's careful work?","John Glenn","Alan Shepard","James Goble","Barack Obama",1],["What had earned that trust?","Her skill and accuracy","Her famous family","Her college team","Her fast speech",1],["What was Katherine's first section at Langley called?","West Area Computing","Space Task Group","Mission Control","Lunar Module Team",2],["During what kind of time did Katherine work?","A time of segregation","A time of peace and equality","A time before computers","A time without rockets",2],["What was Katherine fascinated by from a young age?","Numbers","Airplanes","Painting","Languages",3],["What helped her win allies among her male coworkers?","Her knowledge of analytic geometry","Her skill at sports","Her loud voice","Her fancy title",3],["Who wrote the book Hidden Figures?","Margot Lee Shetterly","Dorothy Vaughan","John Glenn","Alan Shepard",4],["What did the public know about Katherine and her coworkers for many years?","Very little","Everything","Only her name","Only her age",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Katherine Earned Trust","paras":["Katherine's method was careful checking: when John Glenn asked, she checked the computer's numbers by hand. She did not simply trust the machine; she made sure the math was right before anyone's life depended on it. That habit of double-checking helped keep astronauts safe.","Katherine kept learning and sharing her work. She studied new problems in trajectory analysis as space travel grew. Over her career, she helped write 26 research papers. Writing down her work let engineers use and build on her math, and that sharing helped the whole team.","Katherine worked as part of a team. Dorothy Vaughan led the West Area Computing section where she began. Katherine earned a place on the Space Task Group by showing her skill. She learned from skilled people, and she showed others what she could do.","Katherine worked at Langley for 33 years, from 1953 until 1986. During those years, space travel went from a dream to a Moon landing, and Katherine was there for it. Great careers are built one day at a time. She kept showing up and doing careful work, day after day.","NASA honored Katherine by naming the Katherine G. Johnson Computational Research Facility at Langley. Her story inspires students who love math and science. For an athlete, her lesson is about preparation: prepare so well that others can trust you when it counts, whether that means film study, checking your gear, or double-checking your assignment."],"remember":"Katherine Johnson checked every number, kept learning, and earned trust by being ready when it counted.","quiz":[["How did Katherine check the computer's numbers?","By hand","With a bigger machine","By asking a coach","By guessing",0],["Who asked her to check the numbers?","John Glenn","Alan Shepard","Dorothy Vaughan","Barack Obama",0],["How many research papers did she help write?","26","12","60","260",1],["What kind of analysis did she study as space travel grew?","Trajectory analysis","Weather analysis","Sports analysis","Money analysis",1],["Who led the West Area Computing section?","Dorothy Vaughan","Margot Lee Shetterly","Alan Shepard","James Goble",2],["How did Katherine earn a place on the Space Task Group?","By showing her skill","By winning a contest","By writing a book","By flying a rocket",2],["How many years did Katherine work at Langley?","33 years","10 years","20 years","50 years",3],["In which year did she start at Langley?","1953","1943","1963","1973",3],["What was named after Katherine at Langley?","Computational Research Facility","Space Training Center","Mission Control Museum","Rocket Launch Pad",4],["What lesson does the story give athletes?","Prepare so well that others can trust you","Skip details to save time","Let a machine do the checking","Rely on talent alone",4]]}]},{"id":"marshall","name":"Thurgood Marshall","years":"1908-1993","era":"past","tagline":"The lawyer who won Brown v. Board and became the first Black Supreme Court justice","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Boy from Baltimore","paras":["Thurgood Marshall was born on July 2, 1908, in Baltimore, Maryland. His birth name was Thoroughgood, after his grandfather. In second grade, he shortened it to Thurgood. He would grow up to be one of the most important lawyers in American history.","His father, William, worked as a waiter in hotels, clubs, and on railroad cars. His mother, Norma, was an elementary school teacher. So Marshall grew up with a father who served people for a living and a mother who spent her days teaching children.","Marshall went to the Colored High and Training School in Baltimore. It was later renamed Frederick Douglass High School. He graduated in 1925 with honors. Then he went to Lincoln University in Pennsylvania and earned his college degree in 1930.","Marshall wanted to study law at the University of Maryland. But the school did not accept Black students, so it turned him away. He went to Howard University School of Law in Washington, D.C. instead. He worked hard there and graduated first in his class in 1933.","At Howard, Marshall met a law teacher named Charles Hamilton Houston. Houston believed the law could be a tool to fix unfair rules. He pushed his students to work harder than they thought they could. Houston became Marshall's mentor, and the two later worked on big court cases together."],"remember":"Marshall grew up in Baltimore, was turned away by one law school, and became the top student at another.","quiz":[["On what date was Thurgood Marshall born?","July 2, 1908","July 2, 1918","October 2, 1908","January 24, 1908",0],["What was Marshall's birth name?","Thoroughgood","Thorndike","Theodore","Thomas",0],["What job did Marshall's father do?","A waiter","A doctor","A lawyer","A pastor",1],["What was the job of Marshall's mother, Norma?","An elementary school teacher","A hospital nurse","A railroad clerk","A church musician",1],["What was his high school later renamed?","Frederick Douglass High School","Booker T. High School","Baltimore Central High","Lincoln Memorial High",2],["In what year did Marshall graduate from Lincoln University?","1930","1928","1933","1938",2],["Why did the University of Maryland turn Marshall away?","It did not accept Black students","His grades were too low","The class was already full","He lived too far away",3],["Where did Marshall finish in his class at Howard?","First in his class","Third in his class","Tenth in his class","Last in his class",3],["Who became Marshall's mentor at Howard?","Charles Hamilton Houston","Earl Warren","William Marshall","Donald Murray",4],["What did Houston believe the law could be?","A tool to fix unfair rules","A way to win quick fame","A game for rich people","A list of old customs",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"A Lawyer Who Changed America","paras":["Marshall became one of the greatest lawyers in the United States. Over his career, he argued 32 cases before the Supreme Court and won 29 of them. Many of those cases were about equal rights for Black Americans. That record made him famous among lawyers, and it still stands out today.","One early case was for a young man named Donald Murray. The University of Maryland Law School had rejected him because he was Black. Marshall and Houston sued the school in 1935. They argued the rejection broke the 14th Amendment, and the Maryland court ordered the school to admit Murray.","Marshall also won a case in 1944 called Smith v. Allwright. The Supreme Court ruled that Texas could not hold primary elections for whites only. In 1950, he won Sweatt v. Painter, about a Black student who was told he had to attend a separate law school in Texas.","His most famous case was Brown v. Board of Education. On May 17, 1954, the Supreme Court ruled that separate schools for Black and white students were not equal. All nine justices agreed. That meant laws forcing students into separate public schools could not stand.","Later, Marshall became a judge and then a top government lawyer. President Lyndon Johnson chose him for the Supreme Court in 1967. After the Senate approved him, Marshall became the first Black justice in the Court's history. He served until 1991."],"remember":"Marshall argued 32 cases before the Supreme Court, won 29, and became its first Black justice.","quiz":[["How many cases did Marshall argue before the Supreme Court?","32","34","23","40",0],["How many of those cases did he win?","29","22","27","31",0],["Who was the young man in the Maryland case?","Donald Murray","Heman Sweatt","Earl Warren","Charles Houston",1],["Which amendment did Marshall and Houston say the rejection broke?","The 14th Amendment","The 1st Amendment","The 5th Amendment","The 19th Amendment",1],["What kind of election was Smith v. Allwright about?","Primary elections","School board elections","Mayor elections","Jury elections",2],["In which year did Marshall win Sweatt v. Painter?","1950","1948","1956","1960",2],["On what date did the Court decide Brown v. Board of Education?","May 17, 1954","May 17, 1944","June 13, 1954","August 30, 1954",3],["How many justices agreed in Brown v. Board of Education?","All nine justices","Seven justices","Five justices","Only four justices",3],["Which president chose Marshall for the Supreme Court?","Lyndon Johnson","John Kennedy","Harry Truman","Dwight Eisenhower",4],["Until what year did Marshall serve on the Supreme Court?","1991","1981","1975","1993",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Marshall's Timeline","paras":["1908 to 1933: Thurgood Marshall was born in 1908 in Baltimore. He finished high school in 1925 and graduated from Lincoln University in 1930. In 1933, he graduated from Howard University School of Law at the top of his class.","1935 to 1944: In 1935, Marshall and Charles Hamilton Houston sued the University of Maryland on behalf of Donald Murray. Marshall later led the NAACP Legal Defense Fund, a group of lawyers who fought unfair laws. In 1944, he won Smith v. Allwright, a case about voting.","1950 to 1958: In 1950, Marshall won Sweatt v. Painter. Four years later, on May 17, 1954, the Supreme Court decided Brown v. Board of Education. In 1958, he won Cooper v. Aaron, a case about Little Rock, Arkansas, trying to delay letting Black students into its schools.","1961 to 1967: In 1961, President Kennedy named Marshall to a federal appeals court. In 1965, President Johnson made him solicitor general, the government's lawyer before the Supreme Court. Marshall held that job until 1967. In that role, he spoke for the United States in some of the biggest cases of the day.","1967 to 1993: The Senate approved Marshall for the Supreme Court on August 30, 1967, and he took his seat that October. He retired in 1991. He died on January 24, 1993, at the age of 84. He was buried at Arlington National Cemetery, a place that honors many who served the country."],"remember":"From a 1933 law degree to the 1954 Brown ruling and a 1967 Supreme Court seat, Marshall's life moved step by step.","quiz":[["In what year was Marshall born?","1908","1898","1918","1915",0],["In what year did he graduate from Howard University School of Law?","1933","1936","1938","1941",0],["In which year did Marshall and Houston sue the University of Maryland?","1935","1937","1939","1942",1],["Which 1944 case was about voting?","Smith v. Allwright","Sweatt v. Painter","Cooper v. Aaron","Brown v. Board",1],["How many years passed between Sweatt v. Painter and Brown v. Board of Education?","Four years","Two years","Ten years","Eight years",2],["In what year did Marshall win Cooper v. Aaron?","1958","1952","1956","1961",2],["Which president named Marshall to a federal appeals court in 1961?","President Kennedy","President Eisenhower","President Truman","President Hoover",3],["What job did Marshall get in 1965?","Solicitor general","Supreme Court justice","Governor of Maryland","Law school dean",3],["On what date did the Senate approve Marshall for the Supreme Court?","August 30, 1967","May 17, 1954","January 24, 1992","July 2, 1908",4],["How old was Marshall when he died?","84","71","91","67",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Marshall Fought","paras":["In Marshall's time, many places in the United States had laws that kept Black and white people apart. This was called segregation. Black children often went to separate schools, and those schools often had fewer resources. These rules touched almost every part of daily life.","Marshall felt this unfairness himself. When the University of Maryland Law School would not accept him because he was Black, he had to go to a different school. Being turned away was a hard lesson. It showed how unfair rules could block a person's dreams.","Charles Hamilton Houston pushed Marshall to see the law as a tool. If laws were used to hold people back, Houston believed, the law could also be used to open doors. Marshall used this idea again and again in his career as a lawyer.","The 14th Amendment was important to Marshall's work. It says that states must give every person equal protection under the law. Marshall argued that separate schools broke this rule, because separate was not equal. In Brown v. Board of Education, the Supreme Court agreed with him.","Marshall took cases to court one at a time. Each win helped make the next one possible. A single ruling could change a rule for millions of students. His goal was fairness for people who had been shut out, and he kept that goal in mind during every long, slow case."],"remember":"Marshall fought because unfair laws blocked people's dreams, and he believed the law could open doors again.","quiz":[["What was the practice of keeping Black and white people apart called?","Segregation","Integration","Immigration","Registration",0],["What did Black children often attend?","Separate schools","Boarding schools","Night schools","Private tutors",0],["Which school would not accept Marshall?","The University of Maryland Law School","Howard University School of Law","Lincoln University","Frederick Douglass High School",1],["Why would that school not accept him?","Because he was Black","Because he was too young","Because he lived too far away","Because he failed a test",1],["Who pushed Marshall to see the law as a tool?","Charles Hamilton Houston","Donald Murray","Earl Warren","Heman Sweatt",2],["According to Houston, what could the law also be used to do?","Open doors","Raise taxes","Close schools","Win trophies",2],["What does the 14th Amendment say states must give every person?","Equal protection under the law","Free college classes","A seat in Congress","A job after school",3],["Why did Marshall say separate schools broke the rule?","Separate was not equal","They cost too much money","They were too far apart","They had too few teachers",3],["How did Marshall take cases to court?","One at a time","All on the same day","Only in Maryland","Only with the president",4],["What was Marshall's goal?","Fairness for people who had been shut out","Fame for himself","Lower taxes for his state","A seat in the Senate",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Marshall Won","paras":["Marshall prepared hard. He had learned from Charles Hamilton Houston to study the facts and the law until he knew them well. Houston expected his students to work harder than they thought they could. Marshall carried that habit into every case.","Marshall also chose his cases with a plan. He started with cases about graduate and law schools, such as Murray's case and Sweatt's case. Those wins helped build the ground for the case about public schools, Brown v. Board of Education.","He did not work alone. Marshall led a team of NAACP Legal Defense Fund lawyers. Over many years, he argued 32 cases before the Supreme Court and won 29. Even a loss did not stop him from taking on the next case.","Brown v. Board of Education ruled that separate public schools for Black and white students were not equal. In 1967, Marshall became the first Black Supreme Court justice. Today, the main airport near Baltimore, BWI Thurgood Marshall Airport, carries his name.","What can an athlete take from Marshall? Study your game the way he studied the law, and trust your team. And when you lose, get back to work, because each rep builds on the last one. Big results come from small steps taken again and again."],"remember":"Prepare hard, build a plan, trust your team, and keep working after a loss.","quiz":[["Who taught Marshall to study the facts and the law so well?","Charles Hamilton Houston","Earl Warren","Donald Murray","Lyndon Johnson",0],["What did Houston expect from his students?","To work harder than they thought they could","To memorize every old case","To avoid hard cases","To finish before the class",0],["Which kinds of schools did Marshall start with in his plan?","Graduate and law schools","Elementary schools","Sports academies","Art schools",1],["Which later case did those wins help build toward?","Brown v. Board of Education","Smith v. Allwright","Cooper v. Aaron","Murray v. Pearson",1],["Whom did Marshall lead?","A team of NAACP Legal Defense Fund lawyers","A group of Maryland judges","A class of law students","A committee in the Senate",2],["How many of his Supreme Court cases did Marshall win?","29","30","22","15",2],["What did Marshall become in 1967?","The first Black Supreme Court justice","The governor of Maryland","The president of Howard","The mayor of Baltimore",3],["Which place near Baltimore carries his name?","BWI Thurgood Marshall Airport","Lincoln Memorial Stadium","Marshall Harbor Bridge","Fort Thurgood Park",3],["According to the lesson, what should you do when you lose?","Get back to work","Quit the team","Blame the referee","Take a month off",4],["According to the lesson, how should an athlete study the game?","The way Marshall studied the law","Only by playing more games","By watching only highlights","Only during the season",4]]}]},{"id":"morgan","name":"Garrett Morgan","years":"1877-1963","era":"past","tagline":"Inventor of a life-saving safety hood and an improved traffic signal","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Kentucky Boy Who Loved Machines","paras":["Garrett Morgan was born on March 4, 1877, in Kentucky. His parents, Sydney and Elizabeth, had both been enslaved earlier in their lives. Garrett was one of eleven children in the family. He grew up at a time when Black Americans faced many barriers.","Garrett left school early, after about the sixth grade. In his teens, he moved north to Ohio to find work. He first worked as a handyman in Cincinnati. Even though he was no longer in school, he hired a tutor so he could keep learning.","In 1895, Morgan moved to Cleveland, Ohio. There he found a job fixing sewing machines for a clothing company. That job gave him a close look at how machines work. He later ran his own tailoring shop, too, and working with his hands became the base of his career.","Morgan was an inventor from the start. One of his first inventions was a belt fastener for sewing machines. He also invented a zigzag attachment for sewing machines. These small ideas were early steps on the road to his bigger inventions.","Morgan cared about his community. In 1908, he helped start the Cleveland Association of Colored Men, a group that later joined with the NAACP. He also started a newspaper called the Cleveland Call. It gave Black readers in Cleveland news that mattered to them."],"remember":"Morgan left school early but kept learning, and he turned his skill with machines into inventions.","quiz":[["On what date was Garrett Morgan born?","March 4, 1877","March 4, 1887","July 27, 1877","November 20, 1877",0],["How many children were in Morgan's family?","Eleven","Three","Seven","Twenty",0],["About which grade did Morgan finish before leaving school?","The sixth grade","The second grade","The tenth grade","The twelfth grade",1],["In which city did Morgan first work as a handyman?","Cincinnati","Cleveland","Columbus","Chicago",1],["In what year did Morgan move to Cleveland?","1895","1877","1908","1923",2],["What kind of machines did Morgan fix in Cleveland?","Sewing machines","Printing presses","Farm tractors","Railroad engines",2],["What was one of Morgan's first inventions?","A belt fastener for sewing machines","A traffic signal for cars","A hood for firefighters","A lamp for coal miners",3],["What other attachment did Morgan invent for sewing machines?","A zigzag attachment","A speed meter","A foot pedal lock","A thread cutter",3],["Which group did Morgan help start in 1908?","The Cleveland Association of Colored Men","The Ohio Inventors Club","The Kentucky Farm League","The Cleveland Fire Society",4],["What was the name of the newspaper Morgan started?","The Cleveland Call","The Ohio Star","The Daily Signal","The Lake Erie Post",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"Two Inventions That Saved Lives","paras":["Morgan is best known for two inventions that kept people safe. One was a breathing device, often called a safety hood. The other was an improved traffic signal. Both were meant to protect people, and each one grew out of a real problem he saw.","The safety hood fit over a person's head. It had a long tube that reached down toward the floor, where the air is often cleaner during a fire. That let the wearer breathe fresh air while smoke stayed higher up. Morgan received a patent for the device in 1914.","In 1916, an explosion trapped workers in a tunnel being dug under Lake Erie, near Cleveland. Morgan and some helpers wore safety hoods and went into the smoky tunnel. They pulled out men who had been overcome by the fumes. The rescue became a famous story.","Morgan's traffic signal was patented in 1923. Early signals had only stop and go. His T-shaped signal added a third position, a caution warning, like today's yellow light. This gave vehicles already in the intersection time to clear before traffic moved the other way.","Morgan later sold the rights to his traffic signal to General Electric for $40,000. He did not invent the very first traffic signal, though. Others had built earlier ones. His improvement was the added caution position, which made the signal safer."],"remember":"Morgan invented a safety hood that helped people breathe in smoke and a traffic signal with a caution position.","quiz":[["How many inventions is Morgan best known for in this lesson?","Two","Five","Nine","Twelve",0],["What was the breathing device often called?","A safety hood","A fire boot","A diving bell","A smoke lamp",0],["Where did the hood's long tube reach down toward?","The floor","The ceiling","The window","The door",1],["In what year did Morgan receive a patent for the hood?","1914","1895","1923","1943",1],["Where was the tunnel that exploded in 1916?","Under Lake Erie","Under the Ohio River","Under downtown Cincinnati","Under a Kentucky mountain",2],["What did Morgan and his helpers wear into the tunnel?","Safety hoods","Metal helmets","Rubber boots","Wool coats",2],["What third position did Morgan's signal add?","A caution warning","A flashing red light","A turn arrow","A walk sign",3],["What shape was Morgan's signal?","T-shaped","Round","Square","Star-shaped",3],["Which company bought the rights to Morgan's traffic signal?","General Electric","Ford Motor Company","Standard Oil","Western Union",4],["How much did the company pay for the rights?","$40,000","$400","$4,000","$4 million",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Morgan's Timeline","paras":["1877 to 1895: Garrett Morgan was born on March 4, 1877, in Kentucky. As a teenager, he moved north to Cincinnati, Ohio. In 1895, he moved to Cleveland, which became his home for the rest of his life. Cleveland was a growing city full of factories and new ideas.","1908 to 1914: In 1908, Morgan helped start the Cleveland Association of Colored Men. In 1914, he received a patent for his safety hood. During these years, he was also building a business and testing new ideas. Many of his best-known inventions came from this stretch of his life.","1916: In this year, an explosion trapped workers in a tunnel being dug under Lake Erie. Morgan and others wore his hoods and went in to rescue men. The event gave his hood national attention in the years that followed.","1923: Morgan received a patent for his traffic signal in this year. Soon after, he sold the rights to General Electric. By then, cars were filling American streets, and crashes at crossings had become a growing problem that cities wanted to solve.","1943 to 1963: Later in life, Morgan developed glaucoma, an eye disease. By 1943, he was almost completely blind. Morgan died on July 27, 1963, in Cleveland, at the age of 86. He is remembered as one of Cleveland's great inventors."],"remember":"From a Kentucky birth in 1877 to a Cleveland rescue in 1916 and a signal patent in 1923, Morgan made his mark.","quiz":[["In what year was Morgan born?","1877","1887","1897","1916",0],["Which city did Morgan move to in 1895?","Cleveland","Akron","Pittsburgh","Detroit",0],["What did Morgan help start in 1908?","The Cleveland Association of Colored Men","The Cleveland Fire Department","The Ohio Inventors Hall","The General Electric Company",1],["What did Morgan receive a patent for in 1914?","His safety hood","His traffic signal","His zigzag attachment","His hair cream",1],["What happened in 1916?","An explosion trapped workers in a tunnel","A train crash blocked a bridge","A flood covered a city","A factory moved to Kentucky",2],["What did Morgan and others wear into the tunnel?","Hoods","Boots","Gloves","Helmets",2],["Which company bought Morgan's signal rights after 1923?","General Electric","Ford Motor Company","Standard Oil","Western Union",3],["What was becoming a growing problem by then?","Crashes at crossings","Slow railroad trains","Empty city streets","Broken sewing machines",3],["Which eye disease did Morgan develop later in life?","Glaucoma","Pink eye","Color blindness","Cataracts",4],["At what age did Morgan die?","86","63","75","92",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Morgan Invented","paras":["In Morgan's day, workers faced real dangers. Fires and gas explosions could fill a room with smoke. Firefighters and workers often had no good way to breathe in thick smoke, which put them at risk. A tool that helped people breathe could mean the difference between getting out and not getting out.","Morgan thought about how smoke behaves. Smoke tends to rise, while cleaner air stays low. So he designed a hood with a tube that pulled air from near the floor. The idea was simple, and it was built on a real fact about fire.","Why did Morgan go into a smoky tunnel in 1916? Men were trapped and in danger, and he had a tool that might help. Because he had already invented the hood, he knew it could work. He chose to act instead of waiting.","In the early 1900s, cars were joining horses, bikes, and streetcars on the same streets. Early signals with only stop and go could cause crashes, because traffic switched with no warning. Morgan wanted drivers to have a pause between stop and go.","Morgan faced unfair treatment because he was Black. Early news stories often made his part in the tunnel rescue seem smaller than it was. He kept inventing anyway, and he kept working for his community through the Cleveland Call. His work speaks for itself today."],"remember":"Morgan invented to solve real dangers: smoke that blocked breathing and streets that switched with no warning.","quiz":[["What could fires and gas explosions fill a room with?","Smoke","Water","Sand","Snow",0],["What did workers and firefighters often lack?","A good way to breathe in thick smoke","A way to call for help","A map of the building","A place to rest",0],["What does smoke tend to do?","Rise","Sink","Freeze","Spin",1],["Where did the hood's tube pull air from?","Near the floor","Near the ceiling","Outside the building","Under the water",1],["Who was in danger in the 1916 tunnel?","Trapped men","School children","Farm workers","Passing sailors",2],["What did Morgan already have that might help?","The hood he had invented","A boat to cross the lake","A truck full of water","A map of the tunnel",2],["What shared the streets with cars in the early 1900s?","Horses, bikes, and streetcars","Airplanes and trains","Boats and ferries","Buses and trucks",3],["What did Morgan want drivers to have?","A pause between stop and go","A faster green light","A louder car horn","A wider road",3],["How did early news stories treat Morgan's part in the rescue?","Made his part seem smaller","Made his part seem bigger","Left out the tunnel itself","Gave all credit to the mayor",4],["What did Morgan do anyway?","He kept inventing","He stopped working","He moved to Kentucky","He closed his newspaper",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Morgan Got It Done","paras":["Morgan learned by doing. He left school after about the sixth grade, yet he hired a tutor to keep learning. He also learned how machines work by fixing sewing machines for a living. His skill grew from hands-on practice, not from a classroom, and that is a habit any athlete can copy.","Morgan began with a real problem. Smoke made it hard to breathe, so he built a hood to bring in cleaner air. Cars crossed paths with little warning, so he added a caution position to a signal. Each invention answered a clear need.","Morgan protected his ideas with patents. He then sold the rights to his traffic signal to General Electric, so a large company could make and sell it. That choice helped his idea reach many more streets than he could have reached alone.","Today's traffic lights have a yellow light, based on the same idea as Morgan's caution position. His safety hood is seen as a step toward the modern gas mask. People still use tools built on the ideas he worked on over a century ago.","What can an athlete take from Morgan? Find a real problem and work on it. Keep learning, even when school or practice feels hard. And be ready to act when your team needs you, because the moment may not come twice."],"remember":"Learn by doing, solve a real problem, and be ready to act when it counts.","quiz":[["What did Morgan hire to keep learning?","A tutor","A coach","A guide dog","A lawyer",0],["How did Morgan learn how machines work?","By fixing sewing machines","By building cars","By reading magazines","By driving trains",0],["What did Morgan build to bring in cleaner air?","A hood","A fan","A chimney","A window",1],["What did he add to a traffic signal?","A caution position","A bell sound","A second pole","A speed gauge",1],["What did Morgan use to protect his ideas?","Patents","Secret codes","Locked boxes","Trophies",2],["Which company bought the rights to his traffic signal?","General Electric","Ford Motor Company","Standard Oil","Western Union",2],["Which modern light is based on Morgan's caution position?","The yellow light","The green light","The brake light","The porch light",3],["His safety hood is seen as a step toward what?","The modern gas mask","The fire truck","The scuba tank","The space suit",3],["What does the lesson say an athlete should find?","A real problem to work on","A shortcut to winning","A perfect schedule","A new brand of shoes",4],["What does the lesson say to keep doing when practice feels hard?","Keep learning","Take a break","Skip a week","Change teams",4]]}]},{"id":"jemison","name":"Dr. Mae Jemison","years":"born 1956","era":"current","tagline":"Doctor, engineer, and the first African American woman to travel into space","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Curious Kid from Chicago","paras":["Mae Jemison was born on October 17, 1956, in Decatur, Alabama. When she was about three years old, her family moved to Chicago, Illinois. Chicago is where she grew up and went to school. She is still alive today and still works on big ideas.","Her father, Charlie, was a maintenance supervisor. Her mother, Dorothy, was a teacher. Both of her parents cared about learning. Mae read science books from the library as soon as she could read, and she never lost her love of asking questions.","Mae loved to dance. She started ballet at age eight, and she later studied modern dance. She kept dancing through college, too. Dance was not just a hobby for her, and it stayed an important part of her life, even as she studied hard subjects in school.","Mae was a fast learner. She graduated from Morgan Park High School in Chicago in 1973, when she was only 16 years old. Then she went to Stanford University in California. Most students her age were still getting ready for their last years of high school.","At Stanford, Mae earned two degrees in 1977, one in chemical engineering and one in African American studies, and she kept dancing. Later, she earned a medical degree from Cornell. She was a scientist, a doctor, and a dancer all at once."],"remember":"Mae Jemison grew up in Chicago, loved science and dance, and finished Stanford with two degrees.","quiz":[["Where was Mae Jemison born?","Decatur, Alabama","Birmingham, Alabama","Houston, Texas","Atlanta, Georgia",0],["To which city did her family move when she was about three?","Chicago","Cleveland","Memphis","Detroit",0],["What was her father's job?","A maintenance supervisor","A school principal","A bus driver","A police officer",1],["What did Mae read from the library as soon as she could read?","Science books","Comic books","Sports magazines","Cookbooks",1],["At what age did Mae start ballet?","Eight","Three","Twelve","Sixteen",2],["Which kind of dance did she study later?","Modern dance","Square dance","Tap dance","Ice dancing",2],["In what year did Mae graduate from Morgan Park High School?","1973","1956","1977","1981",3],["How old was she when she graduated high school?","16","12","18","20",3],["What were her two degrees from Stanford in?","Chemical engineering and African American studies","Computer science and fine art","Biology and American history","Physics and classical music",4],["From which school did she earn her medical degree?","Cornell","Duke","Howard","Yale",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"Doctor, Astronaut, Teacher","paras":["Mae Jemison became a doctor and then an astronaut. In 1992, she became the first African American woman to travel into space. She flew aboard the space shuttle Endeavour. Her flight showed students everywhere what was possible with hard work and big dreams.","Her mission was called STS-47. It lasted from September 12 to September 20, 1992. Seven crew members flew together, and Jemison was a mission specialist, which means she helped run science work on board. She orbited Earth 127 times in a busy week of science and teamwork.","The shuttle carried a science lab that was built with Japan. Jemison helped with experiments there. She tested a way to make fluids for medicine in space. She also worked on bone cell experiments and a frog experiment that studied how tadpoles grow in space.","Before NASA, Jemison served in the Peace Corps from 1983 to 1985. She worked as a medical officer in the African countries of Sierra Leone and Liberia. She looked after the health of Peace Corps volunteers there. It was a big job for someone so young.","After leaving NASA in 1993, Jemison kept working. She started a company called the Jemison Group. She also began a science camp called The Earth We Share, which is for students ages 12 to 16. Today she still pushes people to think big about science."],"remember":"Jemison was a doctor, a Peace Corps officer, and the first African American woman in space.","quiz":[["What first did Jemison achieve in 1992?","First African American woman to travel into space","First person to walk on the moon","First woman to fly a plane","First doctor to visit a space station",0],["Which space shuttle did she fly aboard?","Endeavour","Challenger","Columbia","Discovery",0],["How many crew members flew on STS-47?","Seven","Three","Five","Eleven",1],["How many times did Jemison orbit Earth?","127","74","92","250",1],["Which country helped build the science lab on the shuttle?","Japan","Brazil","France","Canada",2],["What animal was part of an experiment she worked on?","A frog","A mouse","A spider","A goldfish",2],["During which years did she serve in the Peace Corps?","1983 to 1985","1977 to 1981","1987 to 1990","1992 to 1994",3],["What was her job in the Peace Corps?","Medical officer","Pilot","Math teacher","Truck driver",3],["What company did Jemison start after leaving NASA?","The Jemison Group","The Star Gazers","The Moon Society","The Endeavour Club",4],["Which ages is The Earth We Share camp for?","12 to 16","6 to 9","9 to 12","18 to 22",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Jemison's Timeline","paras":["1956 to 1977: Mae was born in 1956 in Decatur, Alabama. She started ballet at age eight and graduated from high school in 1973. In 1977, she graduated from Stanford University with two degrees. Those early years set the stage for everything that followed.","1981 to 1985: Jemison earned her medical degree from Cornell in 1981. Two years later, in 1983, she joined the Peace Corps. She served as a medical officer in West Africa until 1985. These years made her a working doctor as well as a student.","1985 to 1987: Jemison first applied to NASA in 1985. In 1986, the shuttle Challenger exploded, and NASA paused its selection program. She applied again, and in 1987, she was chosen. About 2,000 people applied, and only 15 were picked.","1992: On September 12, 1992, the space shuttle Endeavour launched with Jemison aboard. The crew returned on September 20, 1992. That eight-day trip made her the first African American woman to travel into space. It was the biggest moment of her career so far.","1993 to today: Jemison left NASA in 1993. In 2012, her group won a bid for the 100 Year Starship project. The project aims to make human travel to other stars possible within the next hundred years, and Jemison still leads it."],"remember":"From a 1956 birth in Alabama to a 1992 shuttle flight, Jemison's path took many years of steady work.","quiz":[["In what year was Mae Jemison born?","1956","1946","1966","1976",0],["In what year did she graduate from Stanford?","1977","1975","1981","1985",0],["In what year did she earn her medical degree?","1981","1979","1984","1987",1],["In what year did she join the Peace Corps?","1983","1982","1987","1992",1],["In what year did Jemison first apply to NASA?","1985","1983","1989","1992",2],["How many people were chosen out of about 2,000?","15","5","50","200",2],["On what date did Endeavour launch with Jemison aboard?","September 12, 1992","September 14, 1992","October 17, 1956","March 4, 1993",3],["On what date did the crew return?","September 20, 1992","September 18, 1992","September 30, 1992","October 17, 1992",3],["In what year did Jemison leave NASA?","1993","1987","1992","1996",4],["In what year did her group win a bid for the 100 Year Starship project?","2012","1992","2001","2020",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Jemison Reached for Space","paras":["Mae loved science from a young age. She read science books as soon as she could read. Her parents cared about learning and supported her. That strong start helped her believe she could aim for something big, even when few people around her had done it.","Her love of space grew as she watched others. In 1983, Sally Ride flew in space. Ride was the first American woman to do it. Jemison was inspired by that flight, and in 1985, she applied to NASA to try for herself.","Jemison also wanted to help people. After medical school, she joined the Peace Corps, where she looked after the health of volunteers in West Africa. Later, she started The Earth We Share, a science camp that invites students to try big science ideas.","Being the first African American woman in space showed many students that space was open to people like them. Her camp for students ages 12 to 16 carries that message forward. It lets young people see that science belongs to them too.","Jemison also looks at the big picture. Her group studies how technology affects people and society. And with the 100 Year Starship project, she wants people to think about traveling to the stars. She believes big questions deserve big effort."],"remember":"Jemison reached for space because she loved science, was inspired by Sally Ride, and wanted to help others.","quiz":[["What did Mae do as soon as she could read?","Read science books","Join a sports team","Write a novel","Build a robot",0],["Who supported Mae's love of learning?","Her parents","Her coach","Her neighbors","Her pilot",0],["Who flew in space in 1983?","Sally Ride","Neil Armstrong","Mae Jemison","Amelia Earhart",1],["In what year did Jemison apply to NASA?","1985","1984","1992","1977",1],["What did Jemison look after in West Africa?","The health of volunteers","A school for dancers","A farm for animals","A rocket launch site",2],["What is the name of her science camp?","The Earth We Share","Space Camp Alpha","Star Builders","The Jemison Games",2],["What did her flight show many students?","Space was open to people like them","Science was too hard for most","Only pilots could join NASA","Space travel was easy",3],["Which ages is her camp for?","12 to 16","5 to 8","18 to 25","30 to 40",3],["What does Jemison's group study?","How technology affects people and society","How to train for sports","How to fix old cars","How to grow food in space",4],["What project does she want people to think about?","Traveling to the stars","Building a bigger school","Making a new language","Mapping the ocean floor",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Jemison Did It","paras":["Jemison built her path one step at a time. She finished high school at 16. She then earned two degrees at Stanford and a medical degree at Cornell. Each step gave her skills that she used in the next one.","When NASA paused after Challenger, Jemison did not give up. She applied again, and in 1987, she was chosen. About 2,000 people applied, and only 15 were picked. Her choice to try again made the difference, because a setback was not the end of her story.","Jemison mixed skills from many fields. She studied engineering, medicine, and African American studies, and she kept dancing. She later started the Jemison Group to look at how technology affects people. She never felt she had to pick just one interest.","Jemison's flight made history. Her camp, The Earth We Share, brings science to students ages 12 to 16. And she leads the 100 Year Starship project, which looks at future travel to the stars. Her work continues today, and she still shares her ideas with students and with leaders around the world.","What can an athlete take from Jemison? Do not let one setback end your goal. Stay curious about more than one thing, because sports, school, and art can all make you better. And choose a big goal, then take it one step at a time."],"remember":"Take big goals one step at a time, try again after a setback, and stay curious about more than one thing.","quiz":[["How old was Jemison when she finished high school?","16","12","18","21",0],["Where did she earn her medical degree?","Cornell","Duke","Harvard","Yale",0],["What did Jemison do when NASA paused after Challenger?","She applied again","She quit science","She moved to Japan","She joined a team",1],["How many of about 2,000 applicants were picked?","15","5","50","500",1],["What did Jemison keep doing while studying many subjects?","Dancing","Boxing","Swimming","Painting",2],["Which company did she later start?","The Jemison Group","The Dance Company","The Starship Club","The Space Bank",2],["What is the name of her science camp?","The Earth We Share","The Space Academy","Camp Endeavour","The Stargazers Club",3],["What project does she lead?","The 100 Year Starship project","The Moon Base project","The Ocean Lab project","The Rocket Games project",3],["According to the lesson, what should you not let end your goal?","One setback","One good game","One new friend","One day off",4],["How should you take a big goal?","One step at a time","All at once","Only on weekends","Without a plan",4]]}]},{"id":"mandela","name":"Nelson Mandela","years":"1918-2013","era":"past","tagline":"Spent 27 years in prison, then led South Africa out of apartheid","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"Who Was Nelson Mandela?","paras":["Nelson Mandela was born on 18 July 1918 in the village of Mvezo, in South Africa. His birth name was Rolihlahla. He belonged to the Thembu people, and his family had royal roots. A teacher at his school later gave him the English name Nelson.","After his father died, Mandela went to live under the care of Chief Jongintaba Dalindyebo, a Thembu leader. He grew up in the chief's household and learned about the history of his people. People expected him to become an adviser to the Thembu leaders one day.","Mandela loved sports. At a boarding school called Healdtown, he spent much of his free time on long-distance running and boxing. Training like this can teach a young person to work hard, stay focused, and keep going when things get tough.","In 1939 Mandela began college at the University of Fort Hare. After his first year, he was suspended because he joined a student protest. He never went back to finish there. It was an early sign that he would stand up for what he believed.","In April 1941 Mandela moved to Johannesburg, the biggest city in South Africa. He found work and later studied law. In the city he met people who wanted to end the unfair rules that kept Black South Africans from having equal rights."],"remember":"Mandela grew up in a royal family, loved running and boxing, and chose a different path in Johannesburg.","quiz":[["When was Nelson Mandela born?","18 July 1918","18 July 1928","11 February 1918","5 December 1913",0],["What was Mandela's birth name?","Rolihlahla","Jongintaba","Dalindyebo","Healdtown",0],["Who took Mandela into his care after his father died?","Chief Jongintaba Dalindyebo","Chief Rolihlahla Mvezo","Oliver Tambo","A Johannesburg lawyer",1],["What did people expect Mandela to become?","An adviser to the Thembu leaders","A professional boxer","A famous long-distance runner","A Johannesburg shop owner",1],["Which two sports did Mandela do at Healdtown?","Long-distance running and boxing","Soccer and swimming","Rugby and cricket","Basketball and tennis",2],["What kind of school was Healdtown?","A boarding school","A law school","A military academy","A small village school",2],["In what year did Mandela begin college at Fort Hare?","1939","1941","1918","1949",3],["Why was Mandela suspended from Fort Hare?","He joined a student protest","He failed his exams","He was caught cheating","He missed too many classes",3],["Which city did Mandela move to in April 1941?","Johannesburg","Cape Town","Durban","Pretoria",4],["What did Mandela study later in Johannesburg?","Law","Medicine","Music","Farming",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"What Mandela Did","paras":["Mandela became a lawyer and a leader in the African National Congress, or ANC. The ANC fought for equal rights for Black South Africans. He helped start its Youth League in 1944. In 1952 he helped lead the Defiance Campaign, which urged people to peacefully break unfair laws.","Mandela and his friend Oliver Tambo opened a law firm in Johannesburg. The firm helped ordinary people who were treated unfairly under apartheid. Apartheid was a system of laws that forced people of different races to live apart and have unequal rights. It shaped almost every part of daily life.","In 1960 police killed 69 protesters at Sharpeville, and the government banned the ANC. In 1961 Mandela helped start Umkhonto we Sizwe, which means Spear of the Nation. It was a secret group that carried out sabotage against government buildings and power sites.","Mandela was arrested in 1962. At the Rivonia Trial, he was found guilty and sentenced to life in prison in 1964. He spent 27 years behind bars in total. He was held first on Robben Island, an island off the coast near Cape Town.","Mandela was released on 11 February 1990. He then worked with President F. W. de Klerk to end apartheid. In 1993 the two men shared the Nobel Peace Prize. In 1994 Mandela became the first Black president of South Africa."],"remember":"Mandela fought apartheid as a lawyer and leader, survived 27 years in prison, and became South Africa's president.","quiz":[["What does ANC stand for?","African National Congress","African Native Council","All National Citizens","Alliance for National Change",0],["What did the Defiance Campaign urge people to do?","Peacefully break unfair laws","Leave South Africa","Join the army","Stop paying for school",0],["Who opened a law firm with Mandela?","Oliver Tambo","F. W. de Klerk","Chief Jongintaba","Umkhonto Sizwe",1],["What was apartheid?","Laws that forced different races to live apart","A law about sports teams","A trade deal with other countries","A school for lawyers",1],["How many protesters were killed at Sharpeville?","69","27","11","96",2],["What does Umkhonto we Sizwe mean?","Spear of the Nation","Voice of the People","Shield of the Country","Army of Freedom",2],["What sentence did Mandela receive in 1964?","Life in prison","Ten years in prison","Exile from South Africa","A heavy fine",3],["Where was Mandela first held in prison?","Robben Island","Pretoria","Mvezo","Fort Hare",3],["On what date was Mandela released from prison?","11 February 1990","18 July 1990","12 June 1964","5 August 1962",4],["Who shared the 1993 Nobel Peace Prize with Mandela?","F. W. de Klerk","Oliver Tambo","Walter Sisulu","Thabo Mbeki",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Mandela's Life in Order","paras":["Mandela was born in 1918. In 1944 he helped start the ANC Youth League. Then, in 1948, the National Party won power in South Africa and passed more apartheid laws. These laws pushed Black South Africans even further from equal rights.","In 1952 Mandela helped lead the Defiance Campaign. In December 1956 he was arrested and charged with treason, along with many others. The long trial finally ended in 1961 with a not guilty verdict. Mandela was free, but the struggle was far from over.","In 1960 police killed 69 protesters at Sharpeville. The next year, in 1961, Mandela helped start Umkhonto we Sizwe. He was arrested in 1962. On 12 June 1964, after the Rivonia Trial, he was found guilty and sentenced to life in prison.","Mandela was held on Robben Island from 1964 to 1982. Then he was moved to Pollsmoor Prison, where he stayed until 1988. His last prison was Victor Verster, where he stayed until 1990. In total, he spent 27 years in prison.","Mandela was freed on 11 February 1990, and in 1993 he won the Nobel Peace Prize. He took office as president on 10 May 1994 and left office in June 1999. Mandela died on 5 December 2013, at the age of 95."],"remember":"Key dates: born 1918, jailed 1964, freed 11 February 1990, president 1994, died 2013.","quiz":[["In what year did Mandela help start the ANC Youth League?","1944","1918","1948","1952",0],["Which party won power in South Africa in 1948?","The National Party","The ANC Youth League","The Thembu Council","The Defiance Party",0],["What was Mandela charged with in December 1956?","Treason","Sabotage","Stealing","Breaking a contract",1],["How did the long trial end in 1961?","With a not guilty verdict","With a life sentence","With a fine","With a ban from the ANC",1],["In what year did Mandela help start Umkhonto we Sizwe?","1961","1960","1962","1964",2],["On what date was Mandela sentenced to life in prison?","12 June 1964","5 August 1962","11 February 1990","10 May 1994",2],["Which prison held Mandela from 1982 to 1988?","Pollsmoor Prison","Victor Verster","Robben Island","Pretoria Central",3],["How many years did Mandela spend in prison in total?","27","18","34","22",3],["On what date did Mandela take office as president?","10 May 1994","11 February 1990","14 June 1999","5 December 2013",4],["How old was Mandela when he died?","95","85","75","105",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Mandela Took a Stand","paras":["Under apartheid, people were sorted by race, and Black South Africans had few rights. Pass laws forced them to carry a pass book to move around the country. Most Black South Africans could not vote in national elections. Mandela saw how unfair this was.","As a lawyer, Mandela met clients who were arrested for being in the wrong place without the right pass. He felt that defending people one by one was not enough. That is why he worked to change the laws themselves.","For years the ANC used peaceful protest, marches, and strikes. On 21 March 1960 police opened fire on a crowd at Sharpeville that was protesting the pass laws. They killed 69 people. Afterward, the government banned the ANC and made its work illegal.","Mandela's prison years were hard. On Robben Island, prisoners did hard labor in a limestone quarry. Even so, the prisoners shared books and taught one another. Some called the prison a university, and together they refused to let their spirits be broken.","When Mandela was released, many people expected him to be angry. Instead, he chose to work with his former enemies. He believed South Africa would not heal through revenge. As president, he set up a commission to look into past human rights abuses."],"remember":"Mandela acted because apartheid was unfair, and he chose peace and teamwork over revenge.","quiz":[["What did the pass laws force Black South Africans to carry?","A pass book","A school report","A work contract","A weapon permit",0],["What could most Black South Africans not do in national elections?","Vote","Run for office","Watch results","Sign petitions",0],["Why were Mandela's clients arrested?","They were in the wrong place without the right pass","They had refused to pay taxes","They had skipped school","They had played sports without a permit",1],["What did Mandela work to change instead of only defending clients?","The laws themselves","The prison rules","The school year","The names of the cities",1],["What had the ANC used for years before Sharpeville?","Peaceful protest, marches, and strikes","Armed attacks","Secret deals","Court fights only",2],["What did the government do to the ANC after Sharpeville?","It banned the ANC","It made the ANC leader","It gave the ANC seats","It paid the ANC",2],["Where did the Robben Island prisoners do hard labor?","In a limestone quarry","On a fishing boat","In a coal mine","On a large farm",3],["What did prisoners do for one another?","They shared books and taught one another","They kept all supplies secret","They held boxing contests","They sent letters daily",3],["What did Mandela believe about healing South Africa?","It would not heal through revenge","It would heal by punishing enemies","It would heal by leaving the country","It would heal on its own",4],["What did Mandela's commission look into?","Past human rights abuses","Sports funding","School test scores","Road building",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Mandela Made It Work","paras":["Mandela stayed true to his goals even when it cost him. In 1985 the government offered to free him if he gave up the armed struggle. He turned the offer down and stayed in prison. He would not trade his principles for his own freedom.","Mandela used routine to stay strong. Each morning he exercised in his cell. He also kept reading and studying. He believed a strong body and a sharp mind could help a person get through very long years of hardship, one day at a time.","Mandela knew that talking mattered. While he was still in prison, he began talks with government officials. After he was freed, he negotiated with President F. W. de Klerk. Their talks helped lead to the 1994 election, open to all South Africans.","In 1995 South Africa hosted the Rugby World Cup. Many Black South Africans saw the Springbok team as a symbol of the old system. At the final on 24 June 1995, Mandela wore a Springbok shirt with the number 6. South Africa beat New Zealand, and Mandela handed the trophy to captain Francois Pienaar.","Mandela served just one term and left office in 1999. He showed that discipline and patience can carry a person through hard times. Athletes can learn from this. Train every day, work with your team, and keep your goal in sight, even when progress is slow."],"remember":"Mandela used discipline, routine, and talking with rivals, and he even used sports to bring people together.","quiz":[["In what year did the government offer to free Mandela if he gave up armed struggle?","1985","1995","1964","1990",0],["How did Mandela respond to the offer?","He turned it down","He accepted it right away","He asked for more money","He left the country",0],["What did Mandela do each morning in his cell?","He exercised","He wrote speeches","He painted","He cooked",1],["What else did Mandela keep doing in prison?","Reading and studying","Writing songs","Playing rugby","Practicing boxing",1],["Who did Mandela negotiate with after he was freed?","President F. W. de Klerk","Chief Jongintaba","Oliver Tambo","Pienaar",2],["What did these talks help lead to?","The 1994 election","The 1995 Rugby World Cup","The Rivonia Trial","The Defiance Campaign",2],["What did Mandela wear at the 1995 final?","A Springbok shirt with the number 6","A suit with a gold medal","A New Zealand jersey","A boxing robe",3],["Which team did South Africa beat in the final?","New Zealand","England","Australia","Japan",3],["In what year did Mandela leave office?","1999","1994","2013","1993",4],["Which two things does the lesson say carried Mandela through hard times?","Discipline and patience","Speed and strength","Money and fame","Luck and talent",4]]}]},{"id":"drew","name":"Dr. Charles Drew","years":"1904-1950","era":"past","tagline":"Four-sport athlete turned surgeon whose blood bank work saved lives in WWII","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"Who Was Dr. Charles Drew?","paras":["Charles Richard Drew was born on June 3, 1904, in Washington, D.C. His father, Richard, worked as a carpet layer. His mother, Nora, had gone to Miner Normal School, which trained teachers. Charles grew up in a close, hardworking family.","Charles was a gifted athlete from a young age. He won swimming medals in elementary school. At Dunbar High School in Washington, he earned varsity letters in four sports, and he also won the James E. Walker Medal for all-round athletic performance.","Drew went to Amherst College in Massachusetts on an athletic scholarship. He played on the football and track teams. People remembered his play for years afterward. He earned his college degree in 1926, and he was already thinking about becoming a doctor.","After college, Drew coached and taught for two years at Morgan College. Then he went to McGill University's medical school in Montreal, Canada. He worked very hard there. In 1933 he graduated second in a class of 137 students, an impressive result.","Drew kept studying even after he became a doctor. He earned a Rockefeller Fellowship and went to Columbia University in New York. In 1940 he earned a doctorate in medical science. He was the first African American to earn that degree at Columbia."],"remember":"Drew was a four-sport athlete from Washington, D.C. who worked his way to a top medical education.","quiz":[["When was Charles Drew born?","June 3, 1904","June 3, 1926","April 1, 1950","June 3, 1940",0],["What did Drew's father do for work?","He was a carpet layer","He was a doctor","He was a coach","He was a teacher",0],["How many sports did Drew letter in at Dunbar High School?","Four","Two","Three","Five",1],["Which award did Drew win for all-round athletic performance?","The James E. Walker Medal","The Spingarn Medal","The Rockefeller Medal","The Amherst Medal",1],["Why was Drew able to attend Amherst College?","He went on an athletic scholarship","He won a science contest","His family was wealthy","He was invited by a coach",2],["Which two teams did Drew play on at Amherst?","Football and track","Swimming and baseball","Basketball and soccer","Tennis and golf",2],["Which university's medical school did Drew attend in Montreal?","McGill University","Columbia University","Amherst College","Howard University",3],["How did Drew finish in his medical school class of 137?","Second","First","Tenth","Fortieth",3],["What did Drew earn from Columbia University in 1940?","A doctorate in medical science","A track scholarship","His first high school diploma","A teaching license",4],["What was special about Drew's Columbia degree?","He was the first African American to earn it at Columbia","He finished in just one year","He earned it while playing football","He earned it in Canada",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"What Drew Built","paras":["Drew became an expert on storing blood so it could be used later to save lives. Hospitals give patients donated blood in a treatment called a transfusion. Plasma is the liquid part of blood, and it lasts longer than whole blood, so it can be stored and shipped farther.","At Presbyterian Hospital in New York, Drew worked with Dr. John Scudder. They studied blood chemistry and how to replace lost fluids in patients. A trial blood bank at the hospital ran for seven months. This work helped Drew learn how to keep blood safe and usable.","In 1940 Drew became medical director of the Blood for Britain program. Germany was attacking Britain, and many people were hurt. Drew set uniform procedures for collecting blood and processing plasma at the New York hospitals taking part. The plasma was then shipped to Britain.","In 1941 Drew helped lead a pilot program for the American Red Cross. It became the model for a national blood donor service. His new ideas included mass-producing dried plasma. He also introduced bloodmobiles, which were vehicles that traveled to collect blood from donors.","Drew went back to Howard University in 1941 and became chair of its surgery department. He also became chief of surgery at Freedmen's Hospital. He trained young surgeons there. In 1948 his first group of surgery residents passed their board exams."],"remember":"Drew helped create modern blood banks, led Blood for Britain, and trained a new generation of surgeons.","quiz":[["What is plasma?","The liquid part of blood","A type of vitamin","A kind of bandage","A hospital tool",0],["Why was plasma useful for shipping?","It lasts longer than whole blood","It is lighter than water","It never needs a donor","It can be made in a lab",0],["Who did Drew work with at Presbyterian Hospital?","Dr. John Scudder","Dr. Richard Drew","Dr. Nora Burrell","Dr. James Walker",1],["How long did the trial blood bank run?","Seven months","Seven years","Three weeks","Two days",1],["What was Drew's job in Blood for Britain?","Medical director","Hospital driver","Army general","Red Cross nurse",2],["Where was the plasma shipped?","Britain","Canada","Washington","Montreal",2],["What new kind of plasma did Drew help mass-produce?","Dried plasma","Frozen plasma","Flavored plasma","Sterile plasma",3],["What were bloodmobiles?","Vehicles that traveled to collect blood","Beds for hospital patients","Boxes for storing plasma","Ships carrying plasma overseas",3],["Which department did Drew chair at Howard University?","Surgery","Chemistry","Athletics","Nursing",4],["What happened in 1948 with his first surgery residents?","They passed their board exams","They moved to Britain","They opened a blood bank","They won a medal",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Drew's Life in Order","paras":["Charles Drew was born in 1904. In 1922 he graduated from Dunbar High School in Washington, D.C. Then he went on to Amherst College in Massachusetts, where he played football and ran track. He earned his college degree there in 1926.","Drew finished medical school at McGill University in 1933. He then worked at Montreal General Hospital from 1933 to 1935, as an intern and surgical resident. In 1935 he came home to Washington, D.C. and joined the faculty at Howard University.","In 1938 Drew received a Rockefeller Fellowship. It let him study at Columbia University and train at Presbyterian Hospital in New York City. He worked there for about two years. In June 1940 he earned his doctorate in medical science.","World War II was raging in 1940. The Blood for Britain program began in August 1940, with Drew as medical director. It ended in January 1941. That same month, Drew began working on a pilot program for the American Red Cross.","In 1944 Drew won the Spingarn Medal, the highest honor of the NAACP. He kept working and teaching in the years after that. On April 1, 1950, he was driving to a medical conference when a car accident took his life, in North Carolina."],"remember":"Key dates: born 1904, doctorate 1940, Blood for Britain 1940-41, Spingarn Medal 1944, died 1950.","quiz":[["In what year did Drew graduate from Dunbar High School?","1922","1926","1933","1904",0],["In what year did Drew earn his college degree from Amherst?","1926","1922","1935","1940",0],["In what year did Drew finish medical school at McGill?","1933","1935","1938","1926",1],["In what year did Drew join the faculty at Howard University?","1935","1933","1944","1950",1],["What did Drew receive in 1938?","A Rockefeller Fellowship","The Spingarn Medal","A Red Cross job","A football scholarship",2],["In which month of 1940 did Drew earn his doctorate?","June","August","January","April",2],["In which month and year did Blood for Britain begin?","August 1940","January 1941","June 1940","April 1950",3],["What began in January 1941, the same month Blood for Britain ended?","A Red Cross pilot program","Drew's Columbia degree","Drew's Howard job","A new football season",3],["In what year did Drew win the Spingarn Medal?","1944","1940","1948","1950",4],["On what date did Drew die?","April 1, 1950","June 3, 1904","January 1, 1941","August 1, 1944",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Drew's Work Mattered","paras":["Wounded soldiers and bombing victims can lose a lot of blood. Doctors needed a way to treat them quickly. Whole blood spoils fast, so it was hard to keep and ship, but plasma could be stored and sent a long way. That is why Drew's research on blood storage mattered so much.","In 1940 Germany was bombing British cities, and many people were injured. Britain needed plasma fast. Drew's Blood for Britain program gave hospitals there a supply they could count on. His careful plan helped doctors treat patients far from where the blood was collected.","The armed forces first would not accept blood from African American donors. Later they said blood from Black donors would be kept separate. Drew spoke out against this rule and called it unscientific and insulting. Drew himself could not give blood to the program he helped create.","Drew wanted to train more young Black surgeons. In that era, many hospitals refused to train them. At Howard University and Freedmen's Hospital, Drew taught surgery and set high standards for his residents. He wanted them to succeed in any hospital.","In 1944 the NAACP gave Drew the Spingarn Medal, its highest honor. Virginia State College gave him an honorary doctorate in 1945, and Amherst College gave him one in 1947. Today he is often called the father of the blood bank."],"remember":"Drew's work mattered because plasma saved lives, and he fought for fair treatment and for training young surgeons.","quiz":[["Why was plasma better than whole blood for shipping?","Plasma could be stored and sent a long way","Plasma was free","Plasma was always type O","Plasma was thicker",0],["What can happen to wounded soldiers that makes blood important?","They can lose a lot of blood","They can forget their training","They can lose their uniforms","They can run out of food",0],["Why did Britain need plasma in 1940?","Germany was bombing British cities","Britain had no hospitals","British doctors were on strike","A flu spread across Britain",1],["What did Blood for Britain give hospitals there?","A supply they could count on","A new set of doctors","A faster airplane","Free training courses",1],["How did Drew describe the rule that kept Black donors' blood separate?","Unscientific and insulting","Necessary and fair","Slow but helpful","Strange but harmless",2],["Why could Drew not give blood to the program he helped create?","He was African American","He was too busy","He was too young","He lived in Canada",2],["What did Drew want to train more of?","Young Black surgeons","Football coaches","Army generals","Hospital cooks",3],["Where did Drew teach surgery?","Howard University and Freedmen's Hospital","Amherst College and Dunbar High School","McGill University and Morgan College","Columbia University and the Red Cross",3],["Which highest honor of the NAACP did Drew receive in 1944?","The Spingarn Medal","The Walker Medal","The Nobel Prize","The Red Cross Award",4],["Which college gave Drew an honorary doctorate in 1947?","Amherst College","Virginia State College","McGill University","Howard University",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Drew Got It Done","paras":["Drew built his work on years of study. His 1940 thesis, called Banked Blood, explained how to preserve blood. Before that, he had worked in a trial blood bank at Presbyterian Hospital. He tested ideas carefully and learned from each trial before he tried something bigger.","Drew made clear rules so that every hospital followed the same steps. In Blood for Britain, he set uniform procedures for collecting blood and processing plasma. Later, he helped lead a Red Cross pilot program that lasted three months. Small tests helped him prove that a bigger plan could work.","Drew spoke up when he saw something unfair. When the armed forces separated donors by race, he said the rule was unscientific and insulting. He used facts and knowledge to explain why the rule was wrong. He did not stay silent.","Drew shared what he knew by teaching. At Howard University, he was chair of the surgery department and chief of surgery at Freedmen's Hospital. His first group of surgery residents passed their board exams in 1948. He helped others do great work after him.","Drew's Red Cross pilot became the model for a national blood donor service. Today, blood banks and bloodmobiles save lives. Drew was a four-sport athlete before he was a doctor. Practice, teamwork, and discipline from sports can help you in anything you choose to do."],"remember":"Drew used careful testing, clear rules, honest speaking, and teaching, skills any athlete can practice.","quiz":[["What was the title of Drew's 1940 thesis?","Banked Blood","Blood for Britain","Plasma Power","The Blood Mobile",0],["Where had Drew worked in a trial blood bank before his thesis?","Presbyterian Hospital","Freedmen's Hospital","Montreal General Hospital","Howard University",0],["What did Drew set so every hospital followed the same steps?","Uniform procedures","Strict curfews","Higher prices","Longer shifts",1],["How long did the Red Cross pilot program last?","Three months","Three years","Seven months","Two weeks",1],["What word did Drew use for the rule that separated donors by race?","Unscientific","Unlucky","Unfinished","Unusual",2],["What did Drew use to explain why the rule was wrong?","Facts and knowledge","Anger and threats","Football skills","Money and power",2],["What job did Drew hold at Freedmen's Hospital?","Chief of surgery","Head of research","Hospital director","Night nurse",3],["In what year did his first surgery residents pass their board exams?","1948","1944","1950","1935",3],["What did Drew's Red Cross pilot become the model for?","A national blood donor service","A football league","A medical school","A British hospital",4],["How many sports did Drew play before he became a doctor?","Four","Two","Three","Six",4]]}]},{"id":"walker","name":"Madam C.J. Walker","years":"1867-1919","era":"past","tagline":"Orphaned at seven, she built a hair care business and gave back to her community","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"Who Was Madam C.J. Walker?","paras":["Madam C.J. Walker was born Sarah Breedlove on December 23, 1867, near Delta, Louisiana. Her parents, Owen and Minerva, had been enslaved on a cotton plantation. Sarah was the first person in her family to be born free. She grew up in a very poor household.","Life became much harder when Sarah was only seven years old. Both of her parents died, and she was an orphan. An older sister raised her after that. Life was hard, and she had very little chance to go to school.","As a young woman, Sarah worked as a washerwoman. That means she washed other people's clothes for pay. She did this work in Vicksburg and later in St. Louis. She was widowed at about age 20 and raised her daughter, A'Lelia, on her own.","Sarah had a problem that bothered her. She had severe scalp trouble and was losing her hair. She tried different hair products that were sold at the time. When they did not work well enough, she decided to make her own product.","Around 1904 Sarah worked as a sales agent for Annie Malone, another Black woman who sold hair care products. The job gave her a close look at how a hair care business worked. Soon she was ready to start one of her own."],"remember":"Born free in 1867 to formerly enslaved parents, Sarah Breedlove faced hardship early and kept going.","quiz":[["What was Madam C.J. Walker's birth name?","Sarah Breedlove","Lelia Walker","Minerva Owen","Annie Malone",0],["In which state was she born?","Louisiana","Colorado","Indiana","Missouri",0],["How old was Sarah when both of her parents died?","Seven","Fourteen","Twenty","Three",1],["Who raised Sarah after her parents died?","An older sister","An aunt","A school teacher","Her grandmother",1],["What job did Sarah do in Vicksburg and St. Louis?","Washerwoman","Teacher","Nurse","Seamstress",2],["What was the name of her daughter?","A'Lelia","Minerva","Annie","Charlotte",2],["What problem did Sarah have with her hair?","Severe scalp trouble and losing her hair","It would not grow longer","It turned gray early","It was too thick to comb",3],["What did she decide to do when other products did not work well?","Make her own product","Stop using hair products","Move to a new city","Ask a doctor for a cure",3],["Whose sales agent was Sarah around 1904?","Annie Malone","Charles Walker","Mary Bethune","Frederick Douglass",4],["What did the job give Sarah?","A close look at how a hair care business worked","A large amount of money","A place to live in Denver","A college degree",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"What Walker Built","paras":["Sarah moved to Denver, Colorado, and started her own hair care business with just $1.50. She made products for Black women, including hair treatments. She sold them door to door. She also went by a new name, Madam C.J. Walker, after she married Charles Joseph Walker in 1906.","As the business grew, Walker took mail orders from customers far away. Her daughter, A'Lelia, filled those orders at the office. Madam Walker traveled and showed women how to use her products. Her demonstrations helped customers see the results with their own eyes.","Walker recruited women to sell her products as sales agents. She trained them and organized them into clubs. She also formed the Madam C.J. Walker Hair Culturist Union of America. The union supported her agents and protected them from imitators selling cheaper goods.","In 1908 Walker moved to Pittsburgh. There she opened Lelia College of Beauty Culture, named for her daughter. The school trained Black women as hair stylists. Graduates of the school could build careers in a field that was growing quickly.","In 1910 Walker moved her company's headquarters to Indianapolis, where she built a factory. Later she built a mansion in Irvington, New York, called Villa Lewaro. She moved in during May 1918. Her business made her one of the most successful Black business owners of her time."],"remember":"Walker started with $1.50, built a hair care company, trained sales agents, and opened a beauty college.","quiz":[["In which city did Walker start her business?","Denver, Colorado","Indianapolis, Indiana","Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania","St. Louis, Missouri",0],["How much money did she start with?","$1.50","$15","$150","$1,500",0],["Who filled the mail orders at the office?","Her daughter, A'Lelia","Her husband, Charles","Her sister","Her sales agents",1],["What did Walker do when she traveled?","She showed women how to use her products","She sold property","She trained athletes","She gave speeches about farming",1],["What did Walker recruit women to do?","Sell her products as sales agents","Teach at her school","Cook in her kitchen","Write her advertisements",2],["Why did she form the Hair Culturist Union?","To support agents and protect them from imitators","To raise prices for customers","To open schools in every state","To stop women from selling",2],["What was the name of the school Walker opened in Pittsburgh?","Lelia College of Beauty Culture","Walker College of Business","Pittsburgh Beauty Academy","Villa Lewaro School",3],["Who was the school named for?","Her daughter","Her sister","Her husband","Her mother",3],["Where did Walker move her company's headquarters in 1910?","Indianapolis","Denver","Pittsburgh","Philadelphia",4],["What was the name of Walker's mansion in Irvington, New York?","Villa Lewaro","Lelia Hall","Walker Manor","Breedlove House",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Walker's Life in Order","paras":["Sarah Breedlove was born on December 23, 1867. This was just a couple of years after slavery ended in the United States. In 1874, when she was seven years old, her parents died. After that she lived with her older sister and had to work hard.","Around 1904 Sarah worked as a sales agent for Annie Malone. In 1905 she moved to Denver, Colorado, to start her own business. In 1906 she married Charles Joseph Walker. From then on, she was known as Madam C.J. Walker.","In 1908 Walker moved to Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. She opened Lelia College there to train hair stylists. Two years later, in 1910, she moved her company's headquarters to Indianapolis, Indiana. This is where she built a factory and grew her business much bigger.","In 1911 Walker gave $1,000 to the building fund for a new Black YMCA in Indianapolis. In 1912 she met Mary McLeod Bethune at a conference of the National Association of Colored Women. Her business was growing and so was her voice as a leader.","In 1917 Walker began organizing her agents into clubs, and the company held its first annual conference in Philadelphia that summer. In May 1918 she moved into Villa Lewaro. On May 25, 1919, Madam Walker died at the age of 51."],"remember":"Key dates: born 1867, Denver 1905, Pittsburgh 1908, Indianapolis 1910, died May 25, 1919.","quiz":[["In what year did Sarah's parents die?","1874","1867","1905","1919",0],["In what year was Sarah Breedlove born?","1867","1874","1906","1910",0],["In what year did Sarah move to Denver to start her business?","1905","1904","1906","1908",1],["What happened in 1906?","She married Charles Joseph Walker","She opened Lelia College","She built a factory","She moved to Indianapolis",1],["In what year did Walker move to Pittsburgh?","1908","1910","1905","1912",2],["Where did she move her company's headquarters in 1910?","Indianapolis, Indiana","Philadelphia, Pennsylvania","Denver, Colorado","Irvington, New York",2],["In 1911 she gave $1,000 to the building fund for what?","A new Black YMCA in Indianapolis","A hospital in Denver","A school in Pittsburgh","A church in St. Louis",3],["Who did she meet in 1912?","Mary McLeod Bethune","Annie Malone","Frederick Douglass","Booker Washington",3],["In which city did the company hold its first annual conference in 1917?","Philadelphia","Indianapolis","Pittsburgh","Denver",4],["On what date did Madam Walker die?","May 25, 1919","May 25, 1918","December 23, 1919","May 23, 1917",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Walker Did It","paras":["In the early 1900s, Black women had few job choices. Many worked as washerwomen, cooks, or maids, and the pay was low. Sarah had worked as a washerwoman herself. She wanted something better for her daughter, and she wanted a better way for other women to earn a living.","Walker's business began with her own problem. She had scalp trouble and was losing her hair. She tried products that were sold at the time, and they did not work well enough. So she set out to make her own, hoping to help women like her.","Walker believed women could build their own futures. By hiring women as sales agents, she gave them a way to earn their own money. She spoke at a National Negro Business League convention. There she urged Black women to create their own opportunities.","Walker wanted to help her community as her business grew. She gave $1,000 to the Black YMCA in Indianapolis and $5,000 to start a campaign for Mary McLeod Bethune's school. She also gave $5,000 to the NAACP's anti-lynching fund. She helped lead a campaign to save Frederick Douglass's home.","Walker thought about how her money could keep helping others. Her will left gifts to the Colored Orphans' Home in St. Louis, the NAACP, and Tuskegee Institute. She also gave money for scholarships, orphanages, and homes for the elderly. Her giving showed what mattered most to her."],"remember":"Walker built her business because she wanted better options for Black women, then shared her success.","quiz":[["What jobs did many Black women have in the early 1900s?","Washerwomen, cooks, or maids","Doctors, judges, or pilots","Bankers, farmers, or sailors","Coaches, referees, or players",0],["What did Walker want for herself and her daughter?","Something better","A bigger farm","A quiet life","A move to Europe",0],["What problem led Walker to start making her own products?","She had scalp trouble and was losing her hair","She could not find a job","She wanted to travel","She lost her family home",1],["What did she want her products to do?","Help women like her","Replace all other products","Sell for the highest price","Make her famous",1],["What did Walker give her sales agents a way to do?","Earn their own money","Move to Europe","Run for office","Skip school",2],["What did she urge Black women to do at a convention?","Create their own opportunities","Wait for better jobs","Save all their money","Leave the country",2],["How much did she give to the NAACP's anti-lynching fund?","$5,000","$10,000","$100,000","$500",3],["Whose home did she help campaign to save?","Frederick Douglass","Booker T. Washington","Harriet Tubman","Charles Joseph Walker",3],["Which institute did Walker's will leave a gift to?","Tuskegee Institute","Hampton Institute","Carver Institute","Dunbar Institute",4],["In which city was the Colored Orphans' Home that she remembered in her will?","St. Louis","Denver","Pittsburgh","Philadelphia",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Walker Made It Work","paras":["Walker started small and kept going. She began with $1.50 and sold door to door. She did not wait for a big opportunity. She went to women's homes and showed them how her products worked, and she turned small sales into a growing company.","Walker also built a team. Her daughter, A'Lelia, filled mail orders at the office. Freeman Ransom ran daily operations while Walker was on the road. Because she trusted others with big jobs, she could travel and meet customers, and a good team lets a leader grow.","Walker organized her sales agents into clubs. In 1917 the company held its first annual conference in Philadelphia. Agents learned from each other and talked about business. Walker believed women could succeed more when they worked together, and she gave them a place to do it.","Walker met other leaders and learned from them. In 1912 she met Mary McLeod Bethune at the National Association of Colored Women conference. She also spoke at a National Negro Business League convention. By speaking up, she shared her ideas and made new connections.","Walker died in 1919, but her work lived on. Her Indianapolis headquarters is now the Madame Walker Theatre Center, a historic building. Athletes can learn from her. Start small, build a team, keep working when things are hard, and use your success to help others."],"remember":"Walker started small, built a team, worked with others, and gave back, habits any athlete can use.","quiz":[["How much money did Walker begin with?","$1.50","$15","$150","$1,500",0],["How did Walker show customers what her products could do?","She went to women's homes and showed them","She gave them a free week","She sold them at stores","She hired models",0],["Who ran daily operations while Walker was on the road?","Freeman Ransom","Mary Bethune","Charles Walker","Annie Malone",1],["What did A'Lelia do at the office?","Filled mail orders","Taught classes","Designed advertisements","Ran the factory",1],["Where did the company hold its first annual conference in 1917?","Philadelphia","Pittsburgh","Indianapolis","Denver",2],["What did Walker believe about women working together?","They could succeed more","They would compete harder","They would work less","They would need fewer jobs",2],["Which leader did Walker meet in 1912?","Mary McLeod Bethune","Frederick Douglass","Annie Malone","Freeman Ransom",3],["Which group's convention did Walker speak at?","National Negro Business League","National Rugby League","National Medical League","National Women's Union",3],["What is Walker's Indianapolis headquarters now?","The Madame Walker Theatre Center","A hair care factory","A women's college","A private home",4],["What advice does the lesson give athletes?","Start small, build a team, and help others","Win at any cost","Only practice alone","Wait for the right moment",4]]}]},{"id":"owens","name":"Jesse Owens","years":"1913-1980","era":"past","tagline":"Won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics and proved hate wrong","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Boy from Alabama Named J.C.","paras":["Jesse Owens was born James Cleveland Owens on September 12, 1913. He came from the small town of Oakville, Alabama. He was the youngest of ten children. His family were sharecroppers, which means they farmed land owned by someone else. Life was hard, and money was tight.","When Jesse was nine, his family moved north to Cleveland, Ohio. Many Southern families made the same move to find better chances. On his first day at a new school, his teacher asked his name. He said \"J.C.\" With his Southern accent, she heard \"Jesse.\" The new name stuck for life.","In Cleveland, Jesse met Charles Riley, his coach at Fairmount Junior High School. Jesse worked after class to help out, so he could not practice with the team. Coach Riley found a fix. He let Jesse train before school instead. Owens always gave Riley credit for his early success.","Jesse's talent showed up fast. At the 1933 national high school championships in Chicago, he long-jumped 24 feet 9 and a half inches. He also tied the world record of 9.4 seconds in the 100-yard dash. His 100-yard dash mark stayed a national high school record until 1967.","After high school, Jesse went to Ohio State University in 1933. He did not get a scholarship, so he worked part-time jobs while he trained and studied. Fans there called him the \"Buckeye Bullet.\" His coach at Ohio State was Larry Snyder. Great things were about to happen."],"remember":"Jesse Owens grew up poor in Alabama and Ohio, but his talent and a caring coach set him on the path to greatness.","quiz":[["What was Jesse Owens's birth name?","James Cleveland Owens","James Alabama Owens","Jesse Cleveland Owens","John Charles Owens",0],["How many children were in the Owens family, with Jesse the youngest?","ten","four","seven","twelve",0],["How old was Jesse when his family moved to Cleveland?","nine","five","twelve","fifteen",1],["What name did his teacher hear when he said \"J.C.\"?","Jesse","Jerry","Jack","James",1],["Who was Jesse's coach at Fairmount Junior High School?","Charles Riley","Larry Snyder","Henry Baker","Frank Wells",2],["When did Coach Riley let Jesse practice?","before school","after dinner","on weekends","during lunch",2],["In which city were the 1933 national high school championships?","Chicago","Cleveland","Columbus","Atlanta",3],["What time did Jesse run to tie the world record in the 100-yard dash?","9.4 seconds","9.9 seconds","10.3 seconds","8.6 seconds",3],["What was Jesse's nickname at Ohio State?","Buckeye Bullet","Cleveland Comet","Alabama Arrow","Buckeye Blaze",4],["What did Jesse do because he had no scholarship?","worked part-time jobs","took out a bank loan","lived with his coach","taught night classes",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"Four Gold Medals in Berlin","paras":["Jesse Owens is best known for the 1936 Olympic Games in Berlin, Germany. There he won four gold medals in track and field. He won the 100 meters, the long jump, the 200 meters, and the 4 x 100 meter relay. His four golds made him the biggest star of those Games.","Before the Olympics, Owens had one of the greatest days in sports history. On May 25, 1935, he raced at a Big Ten meet in Ann Arbor, Michigan. He competed through a sore back. In about 45 minutes, he won four events and set or tied world records.","In Berlin, Owens won the 100 meters on August 3 in 10.3 seconds. The next day, he won the long jump with a leap of 8.06 meters. On August 5, he took the 200 meters in 20.7 seconds. That was three gold medals in just three days.","His fourth gold came in the 4 x 100 meter relay on August 9. Owens ran with teammates Ralph Metcalfe, Frank Wykoff, and Foy Draper. Together they set a world record of 39.8 seconds. Back at Ohio State, Owens had also won eight individual NCAA titles.","Owens's story did not end in 1936. In 1955, President Dwight Eisenhower named him a goodwill ambassador. In 1976, President Gerald Ford gave him the Presidential Medal of Freedom. In 1990, after Owens had died, he was awarded the Congressional Gold Medal."],"remember":"At the 1936 Olympics, Jesse Owens won four gold medals in the 100 m, long jump, 200 m, and relay.","quiz":[["How many gold medals did Owens win at the 1936 Olympics?","four","two","three","six",0],["Which event was a team race?","the 4 x 100 meter relay","the 100 meters","the long jump","the 200 meters",0],["What problem did Owens compete through on May 25, 1935?","a sore back","a twisted ankle","a sore knee","a bad cold",1],["About how long did his big day of four wins take?","about 45 minutes","about two hours","about ten minutes","a whole afternoon",1],["What time did Owens run in the 100 meters in Berlin?","10.3 seconds","9.4 seconds","20.7 seconds","39.8 seconds",2],["How far did Owens leap to win the long jump?","8.06 meters","7.56 meters","8.31 meters","7.82 meters",2],["How many individual NCAA titles did Owens win at Ohio State?","eight","three","five","twelve",3],["What time did the relay team run to set a world record?","39.8 seconds","20.7 seconds","42.1 seconds","36.5 seconds",3],["Which president gave Owens the Presidential Medal of Freedom?","Gerald Ford","Dwight Eisenhower","Franklin Roosevelt","Richard Nixon",4],["Which honor was awarded in 1990, after Owens had died?","the Congressional Gold Medal","the Presidential Medal of Freedom","an Olympic gold medal","a goodwill ambassador title",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Owens Across the Years","paras":["Jesse Owens's story starts on September 12, 1913, in Oakville, Alabama. When he was nine, his family moved to Cleveland, Ohio. That move changed his life, because it led him to Coach Charles Riley and the school track team. From there, his speed took over.","In 1933, Owens starred at the national high school championships in Chicago. That same year, he started at Ohio State University. On May 25, 1935, he had his famous day at the Big Ten meet in Ann Arbor, Michigan, where he won four events.","The 1936 Olympic Games were held in Berlin, Germany. Owens won his first gold on August 3 in the 100 meters. He won the long jump on August 4 and the 200 meters on August 5. His fourth gold came in the relay on August 9.","After Berlin, Owens came home to a parade in New York City. Later, after a tour of Europe, he was suspended from amateur competition, which ended his amateur career. In 1955, President Eisenhower named him a goodwill ambassador, a kind of friendly representative of the country.","Jesse Owens died on March 31, 1980, in Tucson, Arizona, at age 66. Honors kept coming after his death. On March 28, 1990, President George H. W. Bush presented the Congressional Gold Medal in his honor. Before that, in 1976, Owens had received the Presidential Medal of Freedom."],"remember":"From a 1913 birth in Alabama to Olympic gold in 1936, Owens's rise came fast once he found track.","quiz":[["In what year was Jesse Owens born?","1913","1923","1933","1903",0],["How old was he when his family moved to Cleveland?","nine","six","twelve","fourteen",0],["In which year did Owens start at Ohio State University?","1933","1929","1935","1936",1],["On what date was his famous Big Ten meet?","May 25, 1935","August 3, 1936","March 31, 1980","September 12, 1913",1],["On what date did Owens win the 100 meters in Berlin?","August 3","August 4","August 5","August 9",2],["On which day did his relay win come?","August 9","August 3","August 5","August 12",2],["In which city did Owens get a parade after Berlin?","New York City","Cleveland","Chicago","Washington",3],["In what year did President Eisenhower name him a goodwill ambassador?","1955","1936","1976","1980",3],["How old was Owens when he died?","66","52","59","74",4],["In which year did Owens receive the Presidential Medal of Freedom?","1976","1955","1990","1936",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why Berlin 1936 Mattered","paras":["The 1936 Olympics were held in Nazi Germany. Adolf Hitler's government wanted the Games to show that its ideas about race were right. Those ideas were hateful and false. The Nazis wrongly claimed that some groups of people were better than others.","Owens ran straight into that wall of hate and still won. His four gold medals proved the Nazi claims wrong in front of the whole world. Crowds in Berlin cheered for him. His wins showed that talent and effort do not depend on skin color.","Back in the United States, Owens still faced unfair treatment. Segregation was common, and Black Americans had fewer rights. After a parade in his honor in New York, he had to use a freight elevator to get into a reception held for him. President Franklin D. Roosevelt never invited him to the White House.","Owens had trouble earning a living after the Games. After he was suspended from amateur competition, he could not run in the big meets. He took ordinary jobs and raced horses, motorbikes, and cars for money. Years later, he served as a goodwill ambassador for his country.","Why does his story still matter? He showed that a person can be treated unfairly and still stand tall. He won on the biggest stage while facing unfair rules at home. Honors like the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1976 show how the country later honored what he did."],"remember":"Owens won in front of a hateful government, and his victories proved its ideas about race false.","quiz":[["Where were the 1936 Olympics held?","Nazi Germany","Soviet Russia","Great Britain","France",0],["What did Hitler's government want the Games to show?","that its ideas about race were right","that Germany had the best food","that sports should be free","that every country was equal",0],["What did Owens's four gold medals prove?","the Nazi claims were wrong","he was the strongest man alive","Germany had no good athletes","relay races were easy",1],["According to the lesson, what do his wins show does not depend on skin color?","talent and effort","age and height","money and fame","coaches and teams",1],["What did Owens have to use to enter a reception in his honor?","a freight elevator","a back staircase","a side door","a service car",2],["Which president never invited Owens to the White House?","Franklin D. Roosevelt","Dwight Eisenhower","Gerald Ford","George H. W. Bush",2],["What did Owens race to earn money?","horses, motorbikes, and cars","pigeons, bicycles, and boats","dogs, trains, and planes","camels, carts, and kayaks",3],["What was Owens suspended from?","amateur competition","Ohio State University","the Army","his coaching job",3],["What did Owens show by standing tall despite unfair treatment?","a person can be treated unfairly and still stand tall","unfair rules never matter","only coaches decide success","money is the best prize",4],["Which honor did Owens receive in 1976?","the Presidential Medal of Freedom","the Congressional Gold Medal","an Olympic gold medal","an NCAA title",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"How Owens Got to the Top","paras":["Owens built his speed with practice, not luck. Coach Charles Riley let him train before school when he could not practice later. That small choice gave Owens extra time to improve every week. A coach who cares can change an athlete's path. Owens always thanked Riley for his early success.","At Ohio State, Owens trained under coach Larry Snyder. He had no scholarship, so he worked part-time jobs while he trained and went to class. Balancing work, school, and sports took discipline. Even so, he won eight individual NCAA titles in just two years of varsity competition.","Owens did not rely on only one skill. In Berlin, he won the 100 meters, the 200 meters, and the long jump, plus the relay. That means he trained for speed events and a jumping event. In a relay, runners must pass the baton smoothly, and his team set a world record.","Owens stayed focused when conditions were tough. On May 25, 1935, he had a sore back, yet he still won four events in about 45 minutes. In Berlin, he competed under huge pressure with the world watching. He kept his mind on running his own race.","What can you take from Owens? Find a mentor like Coach Riley and listen to their advice. Use every bit of time you have, even before school. Keep going when things feel unfair. In your own sport, small daily work adds up to big results, just as it did for Owens."],"remember":"Owens combined smart coaching, early practice, discipline, and focus under pressure to reach the top.","quiz":[["When did Coach Riley let Owens train?","before school","after midnight","only on Sundays","during games",0],["Who did Owens always thank for his early success?","Riley","Snyder","Metcalfe","Draper",0],["Who coached Owens at Ohio State?","Larry Snyder","Charles Riley","Ralph Metcalfe","Frank Wykoff",1],["How many years of varsity competition did Owens have at Ohio State?","two years","four years","one year","six years",1],["Besides sprints, what kind of event did Owens train for?","a jumping event","a throwing event","a swimming event","a cycling event",2],["What must relay runners pass smoothly?","the baton","the ball","the flag","the torch",2],["What problem did Owens have on May 25, 1935?","a sore back","a broken shoe","a fever","a sprained wrist",3],["How many events did Owens win that day?","four","two","three","five",3],["Who is the lesson's example of a mentor?","Coach Riley","Ralph Metcalfe","Foy Draper","President Ford",4],["What adds up to big results, according to the lesson?","small daily work","one lucky day","buying new gear","skipping practice",4]]}]},{"id":"king","name":"Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.","years":"1929-1968","era":"past","tagline":"Led the civil rights movement with peaceful protest and a dream of fairness","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Minister's Son from Atlanta","paras":["Martin Luther King Jr. was born on January 15, 1929, in Atlanta, Georgia. His mother was Alberta Williams King. His father, Martin Luther King Sr., was a Baptist minister. His father became the senior pastor of Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta in 1931.","Martin grew up in the South during segregation. Segregation was a system of unfair laws that kept Black and white people apart in schools, on buses, and in other public places. These rules treated Black families as if they mattered less. Growing up, Martin saw how unfair they were.","Martin was a bright student. He was only 15 when he passed the entrance exam and started at Morehouse College in Atlanta in 1944. He graduated in 1948 with a sociology degree. Then he went to Crozer Theological Seminary in Pennsylvania to study religion.","King earned a divinity degree from Crozer in 1951. Then he went to Boston University, where he earned his PhD in 1955. That is why many people called him Dr. King. He also married Coretta Scott on June 18, 1953, and they built a family together.","In 1954, King became the pastor of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church in Montgomery, Alabama. He was only 25 years old. He and Coretta later had four children: Yolanda, Martin III, Dexter, and Bernice. Soon he would help lead a major fight for fairness."],"remember":"Martin Luther King Jr. was a bright, well-educated young minister who was ready to lead.","quiz":[["In which city was Martin Luther King Jr. born?","Atlanta, Georgia","Montgomery, Alabama","Memphis, Tennessee","Boston, Massachusetts",0],["What was the name of his father's church in Atlanta?","Ebenezer Baptist Church","Dexter Avenue Baptist Church","Morehouse Chapel","Crozer Community Church",0],["What was segregation?","unfair laws that kept Black and white people apart","a rule that everyone must go to the same school","a sports league for teenagers","a tax on public buses",1],["In which part of the country did Martin grow up?","the South","the West","the Midwest","New England",1],["How old was Martin when he started at Morehouse College?","15","12","18","21",2],["What degree did he earn from Morehouse in 1948?","a sociology degree","a law degree","a medical degree","a history degree",2],["Why did many people call him Dr. King?","He earned a PhD","He worked as a medical doctor","He was a school principal","He was a famous author",3],["When did King marry Coretta Scott?","June 18, 1953","January 15, 1929","October 31, 1954","May 17, 1954",3],["Which church did King pastor in Montgomery, Alabama?","Dexter Avenue Baptist Church","Ebenezer Baptist Church","Morehouse Baptist Church","Boston Avenue Church",4],["How many children did Martin and Coretta have?","four","two","three","six",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"A Leader of the Civil Rights Movement","paras":["Dr. King became the best-known leader of the American civil rights movement. This movement worked to end segregation and unfair treatment of Black Americans. King led with nonviolence, which means working for change without hitting or hurting anyone. He used peaceful marches, speeches, and boycotts.","His first big test was the Montgomery bus boycott. It began on December 5, 1955, days after Rosa Parks was arrested for refusing to follow bus segregation rules. Black riders in Montgomery stopped riding the city buses. The boycott lasted more than a year.","In November 1956, the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that bus segregation was unconstitutional, so the boycott worked. In 1957, King helped form the Southern Christian Leadership Conference, also called the SCLC, and he was elected its president. The group helped organize protests across the South.","On August 28, 1963, King spoke at the March on Washington. More than a quarter of a million people came. There he gave his famous \"I Have a Dream\" speech. He said he dreamed that his children would \"not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character.\"","King's work helped lead to new laws. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 banned many kinds of unfair treatment. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 protected the right to vote. In 1964, King also received the Nobel Peace Prize, a major world honor, in Oslo, Norway."],"remember":"King led the civil rights movement with nonviolence, and his work helped bring new laws to protect equal rights.","quiz":[["Which movement did Dr. King become the best-known leader of?","the American civil rights movement","the American sports movement","the American space movement","the American labor movement",0],["What does nonviolence mean?","working for change without hitting or hurting anyone","changing laws by winning elections only","protecting yourself by any means","waiting quietly for change",0],["Who was arrested days before the Montgomery bus boycott began?","Rosa Parks","Coretta Scott","Alberta King","Lyndon Johnson",1],["How long did the bus boycott last?","more than a year","about one week","about two months","exactly six months",1],["Which court ruled that bus segregation was unconstitutional?","the U.S. Supreme Court","the Montgomery city court","the state court of Georgia","the Boston county court",2],["What group did King help form in 1957?","the Southern Christian Leadership Conference","the Boston University Alumni Club","the Morehouse Debate Team","the Montgomery Bus Company",2],["What was the name of King's famous speech at the March on Washington?","\"I Have a Dream\"","\"We Shall Not Wait\"","\"A Letter to America\"","\"The Road Ahead\"",3],["About how many people came to the March on Washington?","more than a quarter of a million","about one thousand","about ten thousand","about fifty thousand",3],["Which 1965 law protected the right to vote?","the Voting Rights Act","the Civil Rights Act","the Bus Safety Act","the Education Act",4],["In which city did King receive the Nobel Peace Prize?","Oslo, Norway","Atlanta, Georgia","Stockholm, Sweden","Paris, France",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"King's Life in Order","paras":["King was born on January 15, 1929, in Atlanta, Georgia. He started at Morehouse College in 1944 and earned his PhD at Boston University in 1955. In 1954, he had become the pastor of a church in Montgomery, Alabama. A very big year was about to begin for him.","On December 1, 1955, Rosa Parks was arrested in Montgomery. Four days later, on December 5, the bus boycott began. In November 1956, the Supreme Court ruled that bus segregation was unconstitutional. In 1957, the SCLC formed and King became its president.","In 1959, King traveled to India to study the nonviolent methods of Mahatma Gandhi. In April 1963, he was jailed in Birmingham, Alabama, and he wrote a famous letter from his cell. On August 28, 1963, he gave the \"I Have a Dream\" speech at the March on Washington.","In July 1964, the Civil Rights Act became law. On December 10, 1964, King received the Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo, Norway. In March 1965, he led a march from Selma to Montgomery, Alabama. In August 1965, the Voting Rights Act became law.","On April 4, 1968, King was killed in Memphis, Tennessee. He was 39 years old. He had gone there to support Black sanitation workers who were seeking fair treatment on the job. People across the country mourned. The first national holiday honoring him was observed in 1986."],"remember":"King's work ran from the 1955 bus boycott to 1968, and the laws it inspired changed the country.","quiz":[["In which year was King born?","1929","1919","1939","1944",0],["In which year did King earn his PhD?","1955","1948","1951","1964",0],["On what date did the Montgomery bus boycott begin?","December 5","December 1","November 13","August 28",1],["In which year did the SCLC form?","1957","1955","1963","1968",1],["Which country did King travel to in 1959?","India","Norway","Germany","Egypt",2],["In which city was King jailed in April 1963?","Birmingham, Alabama","Selma, Alabama","Memphis, Tennessee","Atlanta, Georgia",2],["On what date did King receive the Nobel Peace Prize?","December 10, 1964","August 28, 1963","April 4, 1968","July 1964",3],["In March 1965, King led a march from which city to Montgomery?","Selma","Memphis","Birmingham","Atlanta",3],["How old was King when he died?","39","29","45","52",4],["In which year was the first national holiday honoring King observed?","1986","1964","1968","1975",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why King Stood Up","paras":["King lived when segregation was the law in much of the South. Black children often had to go to separate schools. Black riders had to follow different rules on buses. These rules were meant to keep Black and white people apart and treated Black Americans unfairly. King believed they were wrong.","King was also a minister. His faith taught him to love others and treat all people with dignity. He believed that fighting hate with hate would only create more hate. So he chose to fight unfair laws with courage, love, and peaceful action instead of violence.","King also learned from Mahatma Gandhi, who led nonviolent protests in India. In 1959, King traveled to India to study Gandhi's methods. King saw that peaceful protest could be a powerful way to change unfair rules. He brought these ideas back to the United States.","In April 1963, King was jailed in Birmingham, Alabama, for taking part in protests. From his cell, he wrote the Letter from Birmingham Jail. In it, he explained why Black Americans could not keep waiting for fairness and why people must act against unfair laws.","Why does King matter today? His peaceful movement helped lead to the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965. These laws gave Black Americans stronger protection from unfair treatment and a stronger right to vote. Americans now honor him with a national holiday."],"remember":"King acted because he believed unfair laws were wrong and that love and peaceful action could change them.","quiz":[["Where was segregation the law in King's time?","much of the South","all of Canada","only in big cities","only in Alabama",0],["What did Black riders have to follow on buses?","different rules","no rules at all","the same rules as everyone","rules about speed",0],["What did King believe fighting hate with hate would do?","create more hate","end the hate quickly","make friends","win the argument",1],["What kind of leader was King, besides a civil rights leader?","a minister","a coach","a judge","a doctor",1],["Who led nonviolent protests in India?","Mahatma Gandhi","Rosa Parks","Coretta Scott","Lyndon Johnson",2],["Which country did King visit in 1959 to study these methods?","India","Norway","Canada","Brazil",2],["What did King write from his cell in Birmingham?","the Letter from Birmingham Jail","the I Have a Dream speech","a book about Atlanta","a letter to Gandhi",3],["Why was King jailed in Birmingham in April 1963?","for taking part in protests","for skipping school","for breaking a bus","for a traffic ticket",3],["Which laws did King's movement help lead to?","the Civil Rights Act and the Voting Rights Act","the Sports Act and the Bus Act","the Land Act and the Trade Act","the School Act and the Park Act",4],["How do Americans now honor King?","with a national holiday","with a yearly football game","with a new school year","with a national sports day",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"Peaceful Protest and Teamwork","paras":["King's main tool was nonviolent protest. In the Montgomery bus boycott, thousands of people simply stopped riding the buses. That action showed the city how much it depended on Black riders. King taught people to stay calm, even when others treated them badly.","King was a powerful speaker. His voice and his words gave people hope. At the March on Washington on August 28, 1963, he spoke to more than a quarter of a million people. He also wrote books and letters, including the Letter from Birmingham Jail.","King did not work alone. He helped lead the SCLC, a group that organized people across the South. Ordinary people, like the bus riders in Montgomery and the marchers in Selma, were the heart of the movement. Big changes came from many people working together.","King kept going even when it was dangerous. He was arrested many times, and his home was bombed in 1956. Still, he kept leading. In March 1965, he led marchers from Selma toward Montgomery, Alabama, to demand the right to vote.","What can you take from King? Stay calm when others try to make you angry. Work as a team, because big goals need many people. Keep going after a setback. On the field, that means controlling your temper, trusting your teammates, and showing up again after a loss."],"remember":"King used peaceful protest, strong words, teamwork, and courage to move a nation.","quiz":[["What was King's main tool for change?","nonviolent protest","angry speeches only","court cases only","private meetings",0],["What did people do during the Montgomery bus boycott?","stopped riding the buses","drove to work together","held a parade","closed the schools",0],["What did King's words give people?","hope","money","free tickets","new jobs",1],["Which famous piece did King write from jail?","the Letter from Birmingham Jail","the Boston University Report","the Atlanta Newsletter","the Selma Song",1],["What group did King help lead that organized people across the South?","the SCLC","Morehouse College","Crozer Seminary","Ebenezer Church",2],["Who were the heart of the movement?","ordinary people","only famous leaders","only politicians","only ministers",2],["What happened to King's home in 1956?","it was bombed","it was sold","it was renamed","it was painted",3],["Why did King lead marchers from Selma toward Montgomery in March 1965?","to demand the right to vote","to win a football game","to start a new school","to visit a church",3],["According to the lesson, what should you do when others try to make you angry?","stay calm","yell back","quit the team","ignore everyone",4],["Why should you work as a team?","big goals need many people","it is always faster","coaches say so","teams never lose",4]]}]},{"id":"obama","name":"Barack Obama","years":"born 1961","era":"current","tagline":"Rose from a Hawaii childhood to become the first Black U.S. president","days":[{"focus":"WHO","title":"A Kid from Hawaii","paras":["Barack Obama was born on August 4, 1961, in Honolulu, Hawaii. His mother, Ann Dunham, was an American. His father, Barack Obama Sr., was from Kenya, a country in Africa. Growing up with parents from two different parts of the world, he learned about more than one culture.","When Barack was six, he moved to Jakarta, Indonesia, to join his stepfather, Lolo Soetoro. He lived there for about four years. At school there, he was known as \"Barry.\" Living in another country taught him about a different culture and way of life.","Back in Honolulu, Barack attended Punahou School on a scholarship. A scholarship helps pay for school. He went there from fifth grade until he graduated in 1979. Punahou is a well-known school in Hawaii. Those years prepared him for college.","After high school, Barack went to Occidental College in Los Angeles on a scholarship. In 1981, he transferred to Columbia University in New York City. He graduated from Columbia in 1983 with a bachelor's degree. Later he would build a career on the skills he learned in school.","Barack married Michelle Robinson on October 3, 1992. They have two daughters, Malia and Sasha. He has also written books about his life, including \"Dreams from My Father,\" which was published in 1995. Today he is a husband, a father, an author, and a former president."],"remember":"Barack Obama grew up in Hawaii and Indonesia, earned scholarships, and built his path through school.","quiz":[["Where was Barack Obama born?","Honolulu, Hawaii","Jakarta, Indonesia","Nairobi, Kenya","Los Angeles, California",0],["Which country was Barack's father from?","Kenya","Indonesia","Hawaii","Canada",0],["To which city did Barack move at age six?","Jakarta, Indonesia","Nairobi, Kenya","New York City","Los Angeles",1],["About how long did he live there?","about four years","about one year","about ten years","about two weeks",1],["In which year did Barack graduate from Punahou School?","1979","1969","1983","1992",2],["How did Barack attend Punahou School?","on a scholarship","by paying in cash","with his stepfather's help","by winning a contest",2],["Which college in Los Angeles did Barack attend first?","Occidental College","Columbia University","Punahou College","Harvard College",3],["In which year did he graduate from Columbia University?","1983","1981","1979","1992",3],["Whom did Barack marry on October 3, 1992?","Michelle Robinson","Ann Dunham","Malia Obama","Sasha Obama",4],["Which book did he publish in 1995?","\"Dreams from My Father\"","\"A Promised Land\"","\"The Audacity of Hope\"","\"Hawaii Boy\"",4]]},{"focus":"WHAT","title":"The 44th President","paras":["Barack Obama became the 44th president of the United States. He took office on January 20, 2009. He was the first Black president in the nation's history. That made his inauguration a historic moment for the country, and people around the world watched it.","Obama won the presidential election on November 4, 2008, defeating Senator John McCain. Voters chose him again on November 6, 2012. In that race, he defeated Mitt Romney. That meant he served two terms as president, which is eight years in office.","Before he was president, Obama worked as a state lawmaker and a U.S. senator. He served in the Illinois Senate from 1997 to 2004. Then he served as a U.S. senator from Illinois from 2005 to 2008. In both jobs, he helped vote on laws.","Obama also worked outside of government. He was a community organizer on the South Side of Chicago from 1985 to 1988. He studied at Harvard Law School and graduated in 1991 with honors. Later he taught constitutional law at the University of Chicago Law School.","In 2009, Obama received the Nobel Peace Prize, a major world honor. It was awarded for efforts in international diplomacy. He left office on January 20, 2017. Joe Biden had been his vice president. Since then, he has written books, including \"A Promised Land,\" published in 2020."],"remember":"Barack Obama served as the 44th U.S. president from 2009 to 2017 and was the first Black president.","quiz":[["Which number president was Barack Obama?","44th","34th","46th","40th",0],["On what date did he take office?","January 20, 2009","November 4, 2008","November 6, 2012","January 20, 2017",0],["Who did Obama defeat in the 2008 election?","John McCain","Mitt Romney","Joe Biden","Ronald Reagan",1],["How many terms did Obama serve as president?","two terms","one term","three terms","four terms",1],["In which state did Obama serve in the state senate and U.S. Senate?","Illinois","Hawaii","California","New York",2],["In which years did he serve in the U.S. Senate?","2005 to 2008","1997 to 2004","2009 to 2017","1985 to 1988",2],["Where did Obama work as a community organizer?","the South Side of Chicago","the north side of Honolulu","downtown Los Angeles","central Jakarta",3],["What subject did he teach at the University of Chicago Law School?","constitutional law","history","math","music",3],["Which prize did Obama receive in 2009?","the Nobel Peace Prize","the Olympic Gold Medal","the Heisman Trophy","the Pulitzer Prize",4],["Who was Obama's vice president?","Joe Biden","John McCain","Mitt Romney","Ronald Reagan",4]]},{"focus":"WHEN","title":"Obama's Path in Order","paras":["Barack Obama was born on August 4, 1961, in Honolulu, Hawaii. At age six, around 1967, he moved to Indonesia for about four years. He then returned to Hawaii. He graduated from Punahou School in 1979 and began his college years.","He transferred to Columbia University in 1981 and graduated in 1983. From 1985 to 1988, he worked as a community organizer in Chicago. In 1988, he began Harvard Law School in Massachusetts. Law school took him until 1991, so those were busy years.","At Harvard, Obama was elected the first Black president of the Harvard Law Review during his second year. He earned his law degree in 1991. From 1992 to 2004, he taught constitutional law at the University of Chicago Law School. In 1992, he also married Michelle Robinson.","Obama served in the Illinois Senate from January 1997 to November 2004. In January 2005, he joined the U.S. Senate. He stayed in that job until November 2008, after he was elected president. His daughters Malia and Sasha were born in 1998 and 2001.","Obama won the presidential election on November 4, 2008. He was sworn in on January 20, 2009. He won again on November 6, 2012. He left office on January 20, 2017, after eight years as president. His time in the White House lasted from 2009 to 2017."],"remember":"From his 1961 birth in Hawaii to the White House in 2009, Obama's path moved step by step.","quiz":[["In which year was Barack Obama born?","1961","1951","1971","1981",0],["In which year did he graduate from Punahou School?","1979","1983","1988","1991",0],["In which year did he graduate from Columbia University?","1983","1981","1985","1991",1],["In which year did he begin Harvard Law School?","1988","1985","1991","1992",1],["In which year did Obama earn his law degree?","1991","1988","1992","2004",2],["In which year did he marry Michelle Robinson?","1992","1991","1998","2001",2],["In which year did Obama join the U.S. Senate?","2005","1997","2009","2001",3],["When did Obama leave the Illinois Senate?","November 2004","January 1997","January 2005","November 2008",3],["On what date did Obama win the 2008 presidential election?","November 4, 2008","January 20, 2009","November 6, 2012","January 20, 2017",4],["On what date did Obama leave office?","January 20, 2017","November 6, 2012","January 20, 2009","November 4, 2008",4]]},{"focus":"WHY","title":"Why It Mattered","paras":["Before 2009, no Black person had ever served as president of the United States. For much of the nation's history, Black Americans faced slavery, and then segregation and unequal rights. Many people saw Obama's election as a major step forward in American history.","Obama's story also showed how education can open doors. He used scholarships to attend Punahou School and Occidental College. He then earned degrees at Columbia University and Harvard Law School. He chose to use what he learned to serve the public.","Obama also made history at Harvard. He was elected the first Black president of the Harvard Law Review. The Harvard Law Review is a respected law journal that is run by students. Being elected its president showed that he had earned respect among his fellow students.","After working as a community organizer on the South Side of Chicago, Obama went to law school. Later he ran for office and won a seat in the Illinois Senate. Then he won a seat in the U.S. Senate. Both jobs gave him a chance to help make laws.","Obama's story matters because it shows that leaders can come from many backgrounds. He was born in Hawaii to a mother from the United States and a father from Kenya. He served two terms and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2009. Historians will keep studying his time in office."],"remember":"Obama became the first Black president, a milestone that many saw as a sign of progress in American history.","quiz":[["Before 2009, what was true about Black Americans and the presidency?","no Black person had ever served as president","one Black person had served four years","two Black people had served as president","a Black person had served one term",0],["What did Black Americans face for much of the nation's history?","slavery, and then segregation and unequal rights","no major problems or limits","only small, short-term troubles","mostly equal chances in life",0],["What did Obama use to attend Punahou School and Occidental College?","scholarships","sports money","gifts from the school","a bank loan",1],["Which law school did Obama graduate from?","Harvard Law School","Columbia Law School","Occidental Law School","Chicago Law School",1],["What was Obama the first Black person to be elected president of?","the Harvard Law Review","the Chicago City Council","the Punahou Student Club","the Columbia Football Team",2],["Who runs the Harvard Law Review?","students","professors only","the president","city leaders",2],["What job did Obama have on the South Side of Chicago before law school?","community organizer","teacher","lawyer","senator",3],["Which two offices did Obama win before he became president?","the Illinois Senate and the U.S. Senate","governor of Hawaii and mayor of Chicago","a judge's seat and a school board seat","a seat in Congress and a mayor's job",3],["How many terms did Obama serve?","two terms","one term","three terms","four terms",4],["In which year did Obama win the Nobel Peace Prize?","2009","2008","2012","2017",4]]},{"focus":"HOW","title":"Building a Path Step by Step","paras":["Obama built his path one step at a time. He worked hard in school and earned scholarships. He graduated from Columbia in 1983. Then he earned his law degree from Harvard in 1991. Each step opened the door to the next one, and none of it happened overnight.","Obama learned to bring people together. As a community organizer from 1985 to 1988, he worked on the South Side of Chicago with the Developing Communities Project. Organizers listen to neighbors. They also bring people together to solve problems. That skill helped him later in his career.","Obama worked on communicating well. He taught constitutional law at the University of Chicago Law School from 1992 to 2004. He also wrote books, including \"Dreams from My Father\" in 1995 and \"The Audacity of Hope\" in 2006. Good speaking and writing helped him share ideas.","He moved up one step at a time in public service. He served in the Illinois Senate from 1997 to 2004. Then he served in the U.S. Senate from 2005 to 2008. After that, he ran for president and won on November 4, 2008. He won again on November 6, 2012.","What can you take from Obama? Work hard in school and in sports. Learn from many different people and teams. Take one step at a time, and keep improving. A big goal, like becoming president, is built from many small steps. Your goals can be built the same way."],"remember":"Obama reached the presidency by working hard, learning from others, and taking one step at a time.","quiz":[["What did Obama earn by working hard in school?","scholarships","trophies","a car","free sports gear",0],["In which year did he earn his law degree from Harvard?","1991","1983","1985","1992",0],["Which project did Obama work with as a community organizer?","the Developing Communities Project","the Hawaii Youth Project","the Harvard Review Project","the Punahou Team Project",1],["What do organizers do?","bring people together to solve problems","make rules for the team","write books about law","work only on their own",1],["What subject did Obama teach at the University of Chicago Law School?","constitutional law","basketball","science","music",2],["In which year was \"The Audacity of Hope\" published?","2006","1995","2020","2009",2],["In which years did Obama serve in the Illinois Senate?","1997 to 2004","2005 to 2008","1985 to 1988","1992 to 2004",3],["On what date did Obama win his second election as president?","November 6, 2012","November 4, 2008","January 20, 2009","January 20, 2017",3],["What is a big goal, like becoming president, built from?","many small steps","one lucky moment","a single big win","waiting for a chance",4],["What does the lesson say to do in school and in sports?","Work hard","Take shortcuts","Wait to be picked","Play it safe",4]]}]}];
